  <section class="introduction">
    <p class="wide">
      Upgrading will mostly mean following a few compiler errors. If you used <code class="inline language-java">EmailBuilder</code>,
      <code class="inline language-java">MailerBuilder</code>, or <code class="inline language-java">ConfigLoader</code> directly, those calls need a
      rewrite. Create one <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code> object for your application and get builders from there.
      Sending calls also change: choose <code class="inline language-java">mailer.sync()</code> or
      <code class="inline language-java">mailer.async()</code> explicitly.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Mail settings are now read once and stay with that <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code> object. Changing a system property
      later will not change builders you create afterwards. Also review validation, shutdown and SMTP compatibility checks below. The other sections
      cover specific uses such as Spring injection, authenticated SOCKS proxies, direct Jakarta Mail sends and raw MIME tests.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Your normal dependency remains <code class="inline language-text">org.simplejavamail:simple-java-mail:10.0.0</code>. You do not need to add Angus
      separately unless your own source code imports Angus classes. This page only covers changes to existing code and behavior. New 10.0.0 features are
      documented on their regular documentation pages.
    </p>
  </section>

