  <a href="#java-11" id="java-11" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Run on Java 11 or newer</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>What breaks:</strong> Simple Java Mail 10.0.0 cannot run on Java 8. All library modules now require Java 11 or newer. The standalone CLI requires Java 17 or newer.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Upgrade the application runtime before moving to 10.0.0. If the application must stay on Java 8, keep using the Simple Java Mail 9.x line.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#builder-entry" id="builder-entry" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Move builder calls to SimpleJavaMail</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>What breaks:</strong> Imports and calls to <code class="inline language-java">org.simplejavamail.email.EmailBuilder</code> and
      <code class="inline language-java">org.simplejavamail.mailer.MailerBuilder</code> no longer compile. Start builders through
      <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code> instead. Removed configuration methods are covered in the matching sections below.
    </p>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java">// 9.x
Email email = EmailBuilder.startingBlank()
        .withSubject("Hello")
        .buildEmail();

Mailer mailer = MailerBuilder
        .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "user", "password")
        .buildMailer();</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-java">// 10.0.0
SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();

Email email = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
        .withSubject("Hello")
        .buildEmail();

Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
        .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "user", "password")
        .buildMailer();</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="wide">
      Import <code class="inline language-java">org.simplejavamail.api.SimpleJavaMail</code> and create it alongside the rest of your application setup.
      Reuse that object. Each call to <code class="inline language-java">emailBuilder()</code> or
      <code class="inline language-java">mailerBuilder()</code> still gives you a fresh builder. The
      <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code> object itself opens no SMTP connections, but each Mailer you build still needs to be closed
      when your application is done with it.
    </p>

