    <p class="wide">
      If you keep a Mailer builder in a variable, use the public interfaces such as
      <code class="inline language-java">MailerRegularBuilder&lt;?&gt;</code> or
      <code class="inline language-java">MailerFromSessionBuilder&lt;?&gt;</code>. The old shortcuts happened to return internal implementation classes.
      The new entry point returns the public interfaces instead.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#explicit-send-mode" id="explicit-send-mode" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Choose whether each call waits or runs in the background</h2>
  <section>
    <p class="wide"><strong>Breaking change:</strong> Replace <code>mailer.sendMail(email)</code> with <code>mailer.sync().sendMail(email)</code> to
      wait for the result, or <code>mailer.async().sendMail(email)</code> to submit background work. Make the same choice for batches and connection tests.
      The choice now belongs to each call, not the Mailer builder. Remove builder <code>.async()</code>; builder and OperationalConfig
      <code>isAsync()</code> are gone too. The direct Mailer methods, including their boolean mode arguments and receipt-specific variants,
      have been removed without forwarding aliases.
      Recompile integrations and custom Mailer implementations against 10.0.0 rather than relying on old binary signatures.</p>
    <p class="wide"><code>sync()</code> and <code>async()</code> use the same Mailer, executor and connections. Neither starts another Mailer or needs separate closing.
      Configuration getters, validation, <code>withOpenConnection(...)</code> and shutdown remain on Mailer.
      The scoped <code>MailSender</code> inside <code>withOpenConnection</code> remains synchronous; its <code>sendMail(email)</code> now returns the receipt
      directly instead of exposing a separate receipt-specific method.</p>
    <div class="wide table-scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Released 9.x call</th><th>10.0.0 call</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>sendMail(email, false)</code>, <code>sendMailAndGetReceipt(email, false)</code></td>
          <td><code>mailer.sync().sendMail(email)</code></td><td>MailSubmissionReceipt</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sendMail(email, true)</code>, <code>sendMailAndGetReceipt(email, true)</code></td>
          <td><code>mailer.async().sendMail(email)</code></td><td>MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt;</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sendMail(email)</code>, <code>sendMailAndGetReceipt(email)</code></td>
          <td>Choose <code>sync().sendMail(email)</code> or <code>async().sendMail(email)</code> explicitly</td><td>Receipt, or receipt-bearing handle</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sendMailsInSimpleBatch(emails, false)</code></td><td><code>mailer.sync().sendMailsInSimpleBatch(emails)</code></td><td>void</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sendMailsInSimpleBatch(emails, true)</code></td><td><code>mailer.async().sendMailsInSimpleBatch(emails)</code></td><td>MailSend&lt;Void&gt;</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sendMailsInSimpleBatch(emails)</code></td><td>Choose the sync or async batch view</td><td>No receipt collection in either mode</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>testConnection(false)</code></td><td><code>mailer.sync().testConnection()</code></td><td>void</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>testConnection(true)</code></td><td><code>mailer.async().testConnection()</code></td><td>CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt;</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>testConnection()</code></td><td>Choose the sync or async test view</td><td>void, or future</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="wide">Every single-email send now produces a receipt. It describes SMTP submission, not final delivery; inspect it only when useful.
      Ignoring a synchronous receipt does not suppress failures. Replace stored asynchronous send futures with
      <code>MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt;</code>, then obtain a <code>CompletableFuture&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt;</code> through
      <code>getCompletion()</code>. Simple-batch completions still carry <code>Void</code>.</p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">// Only completion matters:
mailer.sync().sendMail(email);

// Or retain the exact receipt:
MailSubmissionReceipt receipt = mailer.sync().sendMail(email);

// Background work:
MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; send = mailer.async().sendMail(email);
CompletableFuture&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; completion = send.getCompletion();</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">Synchronous calls throw preparation, connection and send failures directly. Asynchronous calls prepare and validate the email
      when the executor runs the operation. Admission may still wait for executor capacity on the calling thread before returning.
      Preparation, sending and scheduling failures are reported through
      <code>getCompletion()</code>; null arguments still throw immediately.
      SMTP failures use the exception described in <a href="#submission-results">send failures and receipts</a>.</p>
    <p class="wide">Simple batches still consume their iterable lazily, send sequentially over one shared connection and stop on the first failure.
      They return completion only and do not accumulate receipts.</p>
    <p class="wide">Existing executor, pooling and socket-timeout settings, including Spring support, do not select execution
      mode. There was no async-mode property to migrate. If application configuration or runtime data decides the mode, make that branch explicit:</p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">if (runInBackground) {
    mailer.async().sendMail(email).getCompletion().whenComplete(this::recordResult);
} else {
    MailSubmissionReceipt receipt = mailer.sync().sendMail(email);
    recordResult(receipt, null);
}</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">CLI <code>send</code> and <code>connect</code> use the synchronous view and wait for completion.
      Remove <code>--mailer:async</code> and <code>--mailer:async--help</code> when used as execution options; both are rejected with an argument error unless an earlier genuine help option has already ended the command.
      The CLI does not print send receipts, and daemon request concurrency is unchanged.</p>
    <p class="wide">See <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-send-execution">Sending and execution</a> for the current operation contracts.</p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="cli-daemon-startup-configuration">Keep complete configuration when starting a CLI daemon</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Detached daemon startup now retains supported JVM configuration for named Mailers and their locks. Earlier 10.0.0 development builds
      could keep <code>simplejavamail.failover.mailer=standby</code> while dropping <code>simplejavamail.mailers.standby.smtp.host</code>, leaving
      the selected failover undefined. Existing setting names and values need no changes.</p>
    <p>Supply configuration when starting an absent daemon through <code>daemon start</code> or <code>--daemon=acquire</code>. Running instances
      keep their startup defaults; later client JVM properties do not replace them. Supply the complete desired configuration again with
      <code>daemon restart</code> to capture new defaults. Explicit request Mailer options retain the normal conflict policy and lock checks.
      See the <a href="cli.html#daemon-startup-configuration">CLI startup guide</a>. This concerns the unreleased 10.0.0 daemon.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="cli-daemon-shutdown">Account for the CLI daemon shutdown deadline</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Earlier 10.0.0 development builds could accept <code>daemon stop</code> and then wait indefinitely for a stalled command or cached
      Mailer cleanup. The standalone daemon now allows accepted commands 30 seconds to finish within a fixed 60-second overall shutdown limit.
      It interrupts workers after the grace period and terminates its process if cleanup or command code still blocks at the deadline.</p>
    <p>Existing stop and restart commands need no syntax changes. Restart waits for actual exit before starting a replacement, and later startup
      handles any stale discovery state left by forced termination. Preserve uncertain send outcomes instead of assuming mail was unsent or
      automatically submitting it again. Ordinary Java <code>Mailer.close()</code> remains graceful. This corrects the unreleased daemon;
      it adds no released 9.x API migration. See the <a href="cli.html#daemon-shutdown">CLI shutdown guide</a>.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="cli-help-values">Keep CLI values ending in --help</h2>
  <section>
    <p class="wide">Earlier CLI versions silently discarded arguments after any value ending in <code>--help</code>.
      For example, <code>--email:withSubject "How to use --help" --email:withPlainText "Here are the instructions."</code>
      could retain the subject but lose the body and later SMTP or security settings. These values now remain literal,
      and the complete command is processed in order. No new quoting or escaping syntax is required.</p>
    <p class="wide">Review scripts that supplied help-like text: their following options now take effect.
      Actual <code>--help</code>, <code>-h</code> and generated option help such as <code>--email:withSubject--help</code>
      still display documentation without sending and ignore later arguments. A separate argument identical to a registered
      help option keeps that option meaning. Literal values also honor the selected daemon mode, including
      <code>--daemon=require</code>. See <a href="cli.html#section-usage-help">CLI usage help</a>.</p>
    <p class="wide">The same ordering applies to removed options: <code>sjm send --help --mailer:async</code> now shows help instead of a migration error.
      A removed option before help, or without help, still reports its removal. A value consumed by Picocli, such as
      <code>--email:withSubject "--mailer:async=false"</code>, remains literal. No flags or script escaping rules change.</p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="required-mailer-inputs">Use named operations to remove Mailer choices</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Required Mailer setters now reject <code>null</code> immediately with <code>NullPointerException</code> and preserve the earlier selection. Previously a missing CustomMailer,
      OAuth2 token provider or Email template could silently remove it. Check possibly missing inputs before calling the setter rather than
      relying on implicit removal. A missing supplied Session is likewise rejected before replacing the earlier Session.</p>
    <p>For deliberate removal, use <code>clearCustomMailer()</code>, <code>clearOAuth2AccessTokenProvider()</code> and
      <code>clearEmailOverrides()</code>. Use <code>useConfiguredEmailDefaults()</code> to restore property-backed defaults, or
      <code>withoutEmailDefaults()</code> to supply an empty template. Accepted replacements, borrowed-resource ownership and intentionally
      nullable SMTP components keep their existing behavior. See <a href="configuration.html#required-mailer-inputs">required Mailer inputs</a>.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="email-construction">Build Email objects through buildEmail()</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Replace direct <code>new Email(builder)</code> calls with <code>builder.buildEmail()</code>. The constructor is now protected for library
      implementation use. Its former public visibility allowed applications to create plain Email objects that copying and sending could not use.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">// Before
Email email = new Email(builder);

// After
Email email = builder.buildEmail();</code></pre>
    <p>This is a source and binary compatibility break: update direct calls and recompile integrations against 10.0.0.
      An already compiled direct constructor call can fail with an access error. Application Email subclasses are not a supported extension mechanism;
      use the factory's email builders instead.</p>
    <p>The internal bookkeeping interface <code>org.simplejavamail.api.email.EmailWithDefaultsAndOverridesApplied</code> has been removed.
      Remove imports, casts and implementations of it. Applications configure their templates through the Mailer builder and receive the resulting
      Email through ordinary library operations.</p>
    <p>The Email serialized-data format is retained. A stored plain Email remains readable through its getters, but deserialization does not make it
      suitable for operations that require a builder-produced Email. Supply the fields you need from its getters to a fresh email builder before
      sending or converting it; passing the plain object to <code>copying(...)</code> is not a reconstruction route.
      Emails created through the library's builders retain their implementation identity across serialization; exact-message rules still apply.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="email-body-method-names">Use consistent body method names</h2>
  <section>
    <p class="wide">Rename these <code>EmailPopulatingBuilder</code> calls and method references, then recompile against 10.0.0.
      The previous names have been removed. Plain text, HTML text and calendar text now use the same names when setting,
      extending and inspecting their bodies.</p>
    <table class="styled-table wide">
      <thead><tr><th>9.x builder method</th><th>10.0.0 builder method</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>appendText(String)</code>, <code>appendText(File)</code></td><td><code>appendPlainText(String)</code>, <code>appendPlainText(File)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>prependText(String)</code>, <code>prependText(File)</code></td><td><code>prependPlainText(String)</code>, <code>prependPlainText(File)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>appendTextHTML(String)</code>, <code>appendTextHTML(File)</code></td><td><code>appendHTMLText(String)</code>, <code>appendHTMLText(File)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>prependTextHTML(String)</code>, <code>prependTextHTML(File)</code></td><td><code>prependHTMLText(String)</code>, <code>prependHTMLText(File)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>getText()</code></td><td><code>getPlainText()</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>getTextHTML()</code></td><td><code>getHTMLText()</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>getTextCalendar()</code></td><td><code>getCalendarText()</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="wide">This is a naming change. Body content, append/prepend order, absent values and file reading retain their behavior.
      <code>withPlainText</code>, <code>withHTMLText</code>, <code>withCalendarText</code>, the clear methods and the getters on a built
      <code>Email</code> keep their names. The <code>Path</code> variants introduced in 10.0.0 use the new names too; there is no released
      9.x <code>Path</code> overload to migrate.</p>
    <p class="wide">The String append/prepend parameters are declared non-null. A separate validation correction now rejects a null
      fragment before changing the draft instead of concatenating the literal text <code>null</code>. Skip an absent fragment in application
      code; empty strings retain their behavior. Nullable <code>withPlainText((String) null)</code> and
      <code>withHTMLText((String) null)</code> replacements still clear their respective body.</p>
    <p class="wide">Existing CLI options keep their spellings, including <code>--email:appendText</code>,
      <code>--email:prependText</code>, <code>--email:appendTextHTML</code>, <code>--email:prependTextHTML</code> and their
      <code>FromFile</code> variants.</p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="reply-body-alternatives">Review primed reply alternatives</h2>
  <section>
    <p class="wide">In 9.3.6, replying to a single-format original left an empty quote in the missing body alternative. In 10.0.0,
      <code>replyingTo(...)</code> and <code>replyingToAll(...)</code> generate that alternative once while priming the reply. Plain text is
      escaped into HTML with line breaks; HTML produces readable plain text with paragraph/list breaks, link destinations and image alt text.
      Script and style text is omitted, and links/images are not fetched.</p>
    <p class="wide">Review any workaround that supplies or depends on an empty quote. Originals with both formats keep their independent wording,
      and the HTML quote template applies once to the original or generated HTML. Continue editing both alternatives when adding your answer:
      later setters, clears, prepends and Email defaults/overrides keep their existing behavior. No reply method signature changes for this correction.
      See <a href="features.html#section-reply-forward">reply priming and body editing</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="attachment-resource-builder">Build advanced attachments with named metadata</h2>
  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      Replace public <code>AttachmentResource</code> constructors and <code>ofPreEncodedData(...)</code> factories with
      <code>AttachmentResource.builder()</code>. Email-builder attachment and embedded-image overloads that append a description,
      optional transfer encoding or Content-ID have also been removed. Put these choices on the resource builder and add the resource
      with <code>withAttachment(resource)</code> or <code>withEmbeddedImage(resource)</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java">// Before: optional metadata occupies positional arguments.
emailBuilder.withAttachment("report.pdf", source, null, null, "report-42");

// 10.0.0:
AttachmentResource report = AttachmentResource.builder()
    .withName("report.pdf")
    .withDataSource(source)
    .withContentId("report-42")
    .build();
emailBuilder.withAttachment(report);

// Advanced metadata works directly with paths or bytes too.
emailBuilder.withAttachment(AttachmentResource.builder()
    .withPath(Path.of("monthly-report.pdf"))
    .withDescription("Monthly report")
    .build());
emailBuilder.withEmbeddedImage(AttachmentResource.builder()
    .withName("brand-logo.png")
    .withData(logoBytes, "image/png")
    .withContentId("brand-logo-cid")
    .build());</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="wide">
      Short conveniences stay available: <code>withAttachment(path)</code>, <code>withAttachment(name, path)</code>,
      <code>withAttachment(name, dataSource)</code>, <code>withAttachment(name, bytes, mimetype)</code>, and the corresponding
      existing embedded-image and preencoded shortcuts. Lists of resources still work. The CLI retains its existing attachment options,
      including <code>--email:withDescribedAttachment</code> and <code>--email:withEncodedDescribedAttachment</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      <code>withContentTransferEncoding(encoding)</code> asks Simple Java Mail to encode ordinary input.
      <code>withPreEncodedContentTransferEncoding(encoding)</code> declares that input is already encoded and preserves those bytes;
      supplying both is rejected. Content must be supplied before <code>build()</code>. Sources are borrowed by identity, byte arrays
      are copied, and paths remain lazy. <code>resource.toBuilder()</code> retains the source without reading it and lets you change metadata.
      Optional metadata has named getters, clears and resets; clearing or resetting one choice leaves the other choices unchanged.
      Existing Email/attachment serialization remains supported. Recompile consumers and migrate constructor references or subclasses
      explicitly; the removed constructors are not retained as deprecated aliases.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="attachment-content-types">Keep attachment Content-Type parameters</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Attachment conversion now retains valid Content-Type parameters, including <code>charset</code> and format-specific extensions.
      Earlier versions removed them when rendering an attachment or reading it eagerly or lazily. An attachment supplied as
      <code>text/plain; charset=ISO-8859-1</code> keeps that encoding instruction with its unchanged bytes.</p>
    <p>Existing attachment calls stay the same. If your code compares the complete content-type string with a bare type such as
      <code>text/plain</code>, parse the base type when that is what you mean:</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">String baseType = new jakarta.mail.internet.ContentType(
    attachment.getDataSource().getContentType()).getBaseType();
boolean plainText = "text/plain".equalsIgnoreCase(baseType);</code></pre>
    <p>Eager and lazy import retain equivalent type metadata. SJM's selected attachment filename still controls outgoing naming parameters,
      and malformed or injected header content keeps its existing safe fallback. Exact/protected source bytes retain their existing contract.
      See <a href="features.html#section-attachments">adding attachments</a> for a character-encoding example.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="multipart-attachment-import">Keep explicitly attached multipart containers</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Earlier versions recursively opened child <code>multipart/*</code> entities even when marked
      <code>Content-Disposition: attachment</code>. Their text joined the parent body, their nested resources became parent resources,
      and the container attachment disappeared. Import now keeps that explicit attachment as one resource, retaining its filename,
      valid Content-Type parameters, content and existing metadata.</p>
    <p>The conversion calls stay the same. For a message containing a visible body and an attached <code>bundle.mime</code>, inspect the
      body with <code>email.getPlainText()</code> and the complete bundle with <code>email.getAttachments()</code>. If your code depended
      on the bundle's children appearing in parent fields, read the attachment separately with an appropriate MIME parser.</p>
    <p>Ordinary nested body containers and repeated plain/HTML content keep their established interpretation. Inline and filename-only
      multipart containers, effective-root selection, protection processing, attached messages and delivery-report ownership retain their
      existing boundaries. Eager and lazy extraction agree on classification and keep their existing stream ownership; exact/protected
      source bytes remain authoritative under their own contracts.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="calendar-method-import">Review calendar method import</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Earlier versions could reject an otherwise readable calendar when its method value used lowercase or mixed case. Import now recognizes
      existing method names and values without regard to capitalization: <code>method=request</code> and <code>METHOD="rEqUeSt"</code> expose
      <code>CalendarMethod.REQUEST</code>. Content-Type parameters follow the MIME grammar, including quoting and whitespace.</p>
    <p>The conversion calls stay the same. A genuine MIME-header method still takes priority over the calendar body's <code>METHOD</code>,
      and <code>getCalendarText()</code> retains the original body text. Other parameter names such as <code>x-method</code>, or text such as
      <code>name="method=REQUEST"</code>, no longer masquerade as the method parameter; without a real method parameter, the body supplies it.</p>
    <p>Review workarounds that uppercased calendar text before importing it or specifically handled the old case-related failure. Missing and
      unsupported methods retain their existing failure behavior. Existing blank/malformed-header fallback and leading-method-token recovery
      remain, while exact/protected source bytes retain their preservation contract.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="imported-sent-dates">Handle unavailable imported sent dates</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Earlier MIME/EML imports could roll impossible calendar dates into another day or silently treat an unknown timezone as UTC.
      These values now leave <code>getSentDate()</code> and <code>getSentDateAsInstant()</code> null while readable content remains available.
      Keep a null check when using an imported date; valid dates and unambiguous supported legacy formats retain their meaning.</p>
    <p>The conversion calls stay the same. Ordinary reconstruction still generates a current date when none was fixed, while exact EML keeps
      its original bytes. The original <code>Date</code> header is not added to ordinary Email headers. See
      <a href="features.html#interpreted-sent-dates">sent-date interpretation</a>.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="grouped-configuration">Move advanced builder settings into configuration groups</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>The advanced scalar methods on Java Mailer and Email builders have moved into immutable configuration values.
      Their root setter, getter and clear/reset aliases are removed. Common SMTP setup, recipients, content, attachments,
      Email defaults/overrides and the send calls remain directly available.</p>
    <p>For settings used in released 9.x code, move the corresponding Java calls as follows:</p>
    <table><thead><tr><th>Previous root methods</th><th>Value supplied to the root builder</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><code>withConnectionPoolCoreSize</code>, <code>withConnectionPoolMaxSize</code>, pool expiration/claim settings,
        <code>withClusterKey</code> and load balancing</td><td><code>withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig)</code>;
        its builder uses <code>withCoreSize</code>, <code>withMaxSize</code>, <code>withClaimTimeout</code>, <code>withExpireAfter</code>,
        <code>withExpireAfterCreation</code>, <code>withClusterKey</code> and <code>withLoadBalancingStrategy</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>withThreadPoolSize</code>, <code>withThreadPoolKeepAliveTime</code>, <code>withExecutorService</code></td>
        <td><code>withAsyncExecutionConfig(AsyncExecutionConfig)</code>; keep-alive becomes <code>withKeepAliveTime</code> inside the group.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>withProxy</code>, <code>withProxyHost</code>, <code>withProxyPort</code>, credentials and bridge port</td>
        <td><code>withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig)</code>; its builder uses <code>withHost</code>, <code>withPort</code>,
        <code>withUsername</code>, <code>withPassword</code> and <code>withBridgePort</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>withSessionTimeout</code>, <code>withLocalBindAddress</code>, <code>withSmtpClientHostname</code></td>
        <td><code>withConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig)</code>, retaining those setting names.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>trustingSSLHosts</code>, <code>trustingAllHosts</code>, <code>verifyingServerIdentity</code>, custom SSL factories</td>
        <td><code>withTlsConfig(TlsConfig)</code>, retaining those setting names.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>withEmailValidator</code>, <code>disablingAllClientValidation</code>, <code>withMaximumEmailSize</code></td>
        <td><code>withValidationConfig(ValidationConfig)</code>; the warning choice becomes
          <code>withClientValidationWarningsOnly(boolean)</code> on its builder. Validator and size setting names remain.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Automatic embedded-image resolution, bases and containment settings</td>
        <td><code>withImageResolutionConfig(ImageResolutionConfig)</code> on the Email builder.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shared and body-specific content-transfer encoding</td>
        <td><code>withContentEncodingConfig(ContentEncodingConfig)</code> on the Email builder.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ignoringDefaults</code>, <code>ignoringOverrides</code> and per-property template exclusions</td>
        <td><code>withTemplateOptions(EmailTemplateOptions)</code> on the Email builder.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shared Email DSN notification and return options</td>
        <td><code>withSmtpDeliveryOptions(SmtpDeliveryOptions)</code> on the Email builder; Java calls use the typed DSN enums.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
<pre><code class="language-java">mailerBuilder
    .withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
        .withMaxSize(8)
        .withClaimTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(2))
        .build())
    .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
        .withHost("proxy.example.org")
        .withPort(1080)
        .build());

emailBuilder.withTemplateOptions(EmailTemplateOptions.builder()
    .ignoringDefaults()
    .dontApplyOverrideValueFor(EmailProperty.SUBJECT)
    .build());</code></pre>
    <p>Replacing a group replaces its supplied choices. Omitted fields inherit the captured configuration rather than values from
      the previous group. When earlier code set fields in several statements, combine them into one value or edit the existing value:</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(mailerBuilder.getConnectionPoolConfig().toBuilder()
    .withMaxSize(12) // retains the earlier claim timeout
    .build());</code></pre>
    <p>Detailed getters and clear/reset methods belong to each value's builder. On the root builder,
      <code>getConnectionPoolConfig()</code> inspects supplied choices and <code>useConfiguredConnectionPoolConfig()</code>
      restores inheritance for that group; the other Mailer groups follow the same pattern. Read effective settings from the built Mailer.
      Property keys, Spring configuration and the CLI's scalar option syntax retain their contracts.
      See the <a href="/configuration.html#section-programmatic-api-other">current group reference</a> for the complete Mailer groups.</p>
    <p>Repeated CLI builder flags now keep each occurrence's arguments and run as separate calls in command-line order.
      For example, <code>--email:to alice@example.org --email:to bob@example.org</code> adds both recipients.
      Repeated attachment/header additions and replacement or clear/reset operations follow their normal builder behavior.
      See the <a href="cli.html#section-how-to-use">CLI guide</a> for examples.</p>

    <p>The former root <code>disablingAllClientValidation(Boolean)</code> call becomes:
      <code>mailerBuilder.withValidationConfig(ValidationConfig.builder().withClientValidationWarningsOnly(true).build())</code>.
      The meaning of <code>true</code> is unchanged: completeness, address and CRLF checks still run and report findings as warnings.
      <code>false</code> keeps them blocking. Size enforcement and other preparation failures retain their separate behavior.</p>
    <p>Rename <code>OperationalConfig.isDisableAllClientValidation()</code> directly to <code>OperationalConfig.isClientValidationWarningsOnly()</code>.
      The former <code>MailerGenericBuilder.isDisableAllClientValidation()</code> returned the resolved <code>boolean</code> value.
      The new <code>mailerBuilder.getValidationConfig().getClientValidationWarningsOnly()</code> reports the supplied choice:
      <code>null</code> means inherit configuration, so this getter chain is not an equivalent replacement.
      When you need the resolved result, read <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig().isClientValidationWarningsOnly()</code> from an existing built Mailer;
      constructing a Mailer merely to inspect configuration is unnecessary.</p>
    <p>Replace the former root <code>resetDisableAllClientValidations()</code> with
      <code>ValidationConfig.Builder.resetClientValidationWarningsOnly()</code>, applied through <code>withValidationConfig</code>.
      It selects the built-in <code>false</code> fallback; <code>useConfiguredClientValidationWarningsOnly()</code> restores property inheritance.
      The property <code>simplejavamail.disable.all.clientvalidation</code> and CLI options
      <code>--mailer:disablingAllClientValidation</code> and <code>--mailer:resetDisableAllClientValidations</code> keep their spellings and Boolean polarity.</p>

    <h3>Pass typed signing and encryption configurations</h3>
    <p>The released positional <code>signWithDomainKey(byte[], String, String, Set&lt;String&gt;)</code>,
      <code>signWithSmime(File, String, String, String, String)</code> and
      <code>encryptWithSmime(File, String, String)</code> conveniences are removed. Their existing typed configuration overloads remain direct
      on the Email builder; use them as follows:</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">emailBuilder
    .signWithDomainKey(DkimConfig.builder()
        .dkimPrivateKeyData(privateKeyBytes)
        .dkimSigningDomain(signingDomain)
        .dkimSelector(selector)
        .excludedHeadersFromDkimDefaultSigningList(excludedHeaders)
        .build())
    .signWithSmime(SmimeSigningConfig.builder()
        .pkcs12Config(keyStoreFile, storePassword, keyAlias, keyPassword)
        .signatureAlgorithm(signatureAlgorithm)
        .build())
    .encryptWithSmime(SmimeEncryptionConfig.builder()
        .x509Certificate(certificateFile)
        .keyEncapsulationAlgorithm(keyAlgorithm)
        .cipherAlgorithm(cipherAlgorithm)
        .build());</code></pre>
    <p>The example shows independent DKIM, S/MIME signing and S/MIME encryption choices; retain the ones your message already uses.
      The config builders accept <code>File</code> or <code>Path</code>. Store/certificate files are still read immediately and the streams opened
      for them are closed; key bytes are copied. A null algorithm still selects the existing module default. Preserve alias, key/store passwords,
      header exclusions and failure timing. When arguments have side effects, evaluate them in their original order before constructing the value;
      do not move file reads earlier than those evaluations. This does not change signing/encryption policy or introduce a fallback.</p>
    <p>Known I/O failures from these complete key-material reads now use <code>MailException</code> with kind <code>PREPARATION</code>,
      retaining the original cause and suppressed close errors. Update handlers that expected <code>IllegalStateException</code>.
      The released <code>Pkcs12ConfigBuilder.pkcs12Store(InputStream)</code> no longer declares checked <code>IOException</code>.
      An I/O-only catch around that call can become unreachable; retain checked handling for application-owned stream opening, reading or closing.
      Supplied streams remain open, and invalid arguments or malformed cryptographic input retain their separate contracts.</p>
    <p><code>Pkcs12Config.build()</code> now requires store bytes, both passwords and a key alias. Earlier incomplete values could be built,
      then fail in their getters or during use. Supply every choice before building; <code>""</code> or an empty <code>char[]</code> explicitly
      selects an empty password. Required null setter arguments now throw <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> without replacing an earlier choice.
      If you only need to inspect the store bytes, use <code>Pkcs12Config.builder().pkcs12Store(path).getPkcs12StoreData()</code> without building
      an unfinished configuration. Actual store and key validation remains at S/MIME use; passwords and aliases are never inferred.</p>
    <p>Passing null to <code>signWithSmime(SmimeSigningConfig)</code> or <code>encryptWithSmime(SmimeEncryptionConfig)</code> now throws
      <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> before changing an earlier choice. Released callers that deliberately used null to clear one choice
      should use <code>clearSmimeSigning()</code> or <code>clearSmimeEncryption()</code>, respectively; <code>clearSmime()</code> removes both.
      A possibly missing configuration needs an explicit application decision rather than silent removal. Optional algorithm nulls still
      select their documented module defaults.</p>
    <p>The nested configuration setters <code>SmimeSigningConfigBuilder.pkcs12Config(Pkcs12Config)</code> and
      <code>SmimeEncryptionConfigBuilder.x509Certificate(X509Certificate)</code> also reject null immediately with
      <code>IllegalArgumentException</code>, keeping earlier material and algorithm preferences. Previously they stored null and
      <code>build()</code> rejected the missing material later. Handle missing key or certificate lookups before calling these setters;
      there is no required-material clear operation. Optional algorithm nulls retain their module-default meaning.</p>
    <p>The recipient setters <code>withSmimeCertificate(...)</code>, <code>withDefaultSmimeCertificate(...)</code> and
      <code>withFixedSmimeCertificate(...)</code> likewise reject null before changing the certificate or group policy.
      Use <code>clearingSmimeCertificate()</code> to remove an individual recipient's certificate. For group calls, choose
      <code>clearingSmimeCertificates()</code> to strip all certificates, or <code>resetSmimeCertificatePolicy()</code> to stop applying the group rule
      while preserving the original recipients' certificates. A possibly missing certificate now needs an explicit application decision.
      See the <a href="features.html#section-recipient-builders">recipient builder guide</a>.</p>

    <p>Java Mailer builders now take <code>SessionDebugConfig</code>, <code>MailSendTelemetryConfig</code> and <code>MailSendHandlers</code>.
      The old flat setters and observer registrations have been removed. Configure related choices together, then supply the immutable group:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-java">mailerBuilder
    .withDebugConfig(SessionDebugConfig.builder()
        .withEnabled(true)
        .withOutput(SessionDebugOutput.SLF4J)
        .build())
    .withTelemetryConfig(MailSendTelemetryConfig.builder()
        .withEnabled(true)
        .withIntegration(OtelAdapter.wrap(openTelemetry))
        .build())
    .withSendHandlers(MailSendHandlers.builder()
        .withResultObserver(resultObserver, observationExecutor)
        .withProgressObserver(progressObserver)
        .build());</code></pre>
    <p>For debug and telemetry, omitted choices inherit the factory's captured configuration. Replacing a group replaces supplied choices;
      omitted fields do not retain values from the previous group. Edit a group through <code>toBuilder()</code> when retaining those values matters.
      Group getters inspect supplied choices; the groups on <code>OperationalConfig</code> contain resolved choices.
      <code>useConfiguredDebugConfig()</code> and <code>useConfiguredTelemetryConfig()</code> restore whole-group inheritance.</p>
    <p>Telemetry activation is independent of its integration: <code>withIntegration(...)</code> and <code>clearIntegration()</code> do not
      turn it on or off. Set <code>withEnabled(true)</code> explicitly or inherit an enabled property. Enabled telemetry without an explicit
      integration uses the application's global OpenTelemetry instance. Streams, callbacks and executors remain application-owned.</p>
    <p>Move independent observer clears to <code>MailSendHandlers.Builder.clearResultObserver()</code> and <code>clearProgressObserver()</code>,
      or remove the whole fixed group with <code>clearSendHandlers()</code>. Per-send context belongs in
      <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-mail-send-observer"><code>withSendHandlersFactory(...)</code></a>.
      Existing scalar property keys and CLI debug/telemetry options retain their names and meanings.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="duration-settings">Pass Duration values for time settings</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Java time settings, their getters, configuration constructors and published constants now use <code>Duration</code>.
      Methods whose names ended in <code>Millis</code> drop that suffix:</p>
    <table><thead><tr><th>Previous root call</th><th>Group builder</th><th>10.0.0 group call</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><code>withSessionTimeout(1000)</code></td><td><code>ConnectionConfig.Builder</code></td><td><code>withSessionTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(1))</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>withThreadPoolKeepAliveTime(5000)</code></td><td><code>AsyncExecutionConfig.Builder</code></td><td><code>withKeepAliveTime(Duration.ofSeconds(5))</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>withConnectionPoolClaimTimeoutMillis(60000)</code></td><td><code>ConnectionPoolConfig.Builder</code></td><td><code>withClaimTimeout(Duration.ofMinutes(1))</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>withConnectionPoolExpireAfterMillis(30000)</code></td><td><code>ConnectionPoolConfig.Builder</code></td><td><code>withExpireAfter(Duration.ofSeconds(30))</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>withConnectionPoolExpireAfterCreationMillis(300000)</code></td><td><code>ConnectionPoolConfig.Builder</code></td><td><code>withExpireAfterCreation(Duration.ofMinutes(5))</code></td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <p>Defaults, supported zero values and optional settings retain their meanings. Fractional milliseconds and overflow are rejected rather than
      truncated or wrapped. Property files and existing CLI options still use numeric milliseconds; do not change those to ISO duration strings.</p>
    <p>Explicit worker count and keep-alive settings now apply even without the batch module. Previously that configuration could be ignored.
      Unspecified worker defaults remain one without batch and ten with batch, and caller-supplied executors remain application-owned.</p>
    <h3 id="socket-timeout-precedence">Review raw socket timeout properties</h3>
    <p>Earlier releases could replace additional Jakarta Mail timeout properties with the common SJM timeout when creating a Session.
      Explicit raw <code>mail.smtp.connectiontimeout</code>, <code>mail.smtp.timeout</code> and <code>mail.smtp.writetimeout</code>
      values can now replace built-in SJM defaults for their respective operations. The corresponding <code>mail.smtps.*</code> properties
      follow the same rule. Different explicit declarations are subject to the <a href="#connection-conflicts">connection conflict policy</a>.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-java">mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withProperty("mail.smtp.connectiontimeout", 10_000)
    .withProperty("mail.smtp.timeout", 30_000);
// With otherwise default configuration: connect 10s, read 30s, write 60s.</code></pre>
    <p>Review applications that relied on the previous overwrite. An explicit
      <code>ConnectionConfig.Builder.withSessionTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(20))</code> deliberately selects all three timeouts.
      Under the strict default, those values must agree with other explicit declarations; with <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code>,
      the typed choices take precedence over raw properties. For independent typed choices, use <code>withConnectTimeout(...)</code>,
      <code>withReadTimeout(...)</code> and <code>withWriteTimeout(...)</code> inside the same group. Each typed choice affects only its
      operation; within the group, the last setter affecting an operation wins. Mandatory locks remain enforced. See the
      <a href="/configuration.html#socket-timeouts">current timeout guide</a> for inheritance, reset and inspection examples.</p>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#connection-conflicts" id="connection-conflicts" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Review conflicting connection configuration</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>The unreleased 10.0.0 line defaults to <code>ConnectionConflictPolicy.REJECT_CONFLICTS</code>. Earlier releases could silently select
        one of several different connection declarations. For example, raw SOCKS properties could replace a proxy chosen through SJM,
        leaving proxy inspection and the actual connection using different endpoints.</p>
      <p>Different explicit factory, Session, recognized raw-property and Java declarations for the same supported connection setting now fail construction.
        Review deployment settings alongside application code before upgrading. Normal source precedence, equivalent values and ordinary builder editing remain valid;
        see <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#connection-conflicts">the complete connection rules</a> for comparison and Session behavior.</p>
      <p>Review failures with the owners of both application code and deployment configuration. Remove obsolete declarations or align their values.
        If replacement is intentional, explicitly select <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code> on the Mailer builder or through
        <code>simplejavamail.connection.conflictpolicy</code>. Do not add that mode automatically during migration: it chooses which declaration
        controls the connection.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">// After reviewing the intended route:
Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withConnectionConflictPolicy(
        ConnectionConflictPolicy.ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES)
    .withProperties(sharedProperties)
    .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
        .withHost("chosen-proxy.example.org")
        .build())
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
      <p>The override policy gives explicit typed Java members precedence over raw counterparts. Review any deliberate removal as well as replacement;
        <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#explicit-connection-clears">clearing an imported setting</a> is a conflict in strict mode.
        Existing value locks and mandatory TLS rules apply in both modes. A deployment can require strict mode with
        <code>simplejavamail.locked.connection.conflictpolicy=REJECT_CONFLICTS</code>.</p>
      <p>Unknown provider extensions and hidden Authenticator state are outside this comparison. See the
        <a href="understanding-configuration.html#connection-conflicts">advanced configuration guide</a> for policy inspection, reset and restore operations,
        CLI syntax and supplied-Session behavior.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#config-loader" id="config-loader" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Replace static ConfigLoader calls</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>What breaks:</strong> Static calls such as <code class="inline language-java">ConfigLoader.loadProperties(...)</code>,
      <code class="inline language-java">getProperty(...)</code>, and <code class="inline language-java">valueOrProperty(...)</code> no longer compile.
      Load the settings into a <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig</code> object instead, then give that object to
      <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java">// 9.x
ConfigLoader.loadProperties(applicationProperties, false);
Mailer mailer = MailerBuilder.buildMailer();</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-java">// 10.0.0
SimpleJavaMailConfig config = ConfigLoader.builder()
        .withProperties("application", applicationProperties)
        .withEnvironmentVariables()
        .withSystemProperties()
        .load();

SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(config);
Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder().buildMailer();</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="wide">
      The example includes environment variables and system properties because the old
      <code class="inline language-java">loadProperties(...)</code> method always read them too. If you only want the Properties object you supplied,
      simply leave those two lines out.
    </p>

    <h3>Old loading calls</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
      <tr><th>9.x call or result</th><th>10.0.0 replacement</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code class="inline language-java">loadProperties(filename, false)</code></td>
        <td><code class="inline language-java">builder().withClasspathResource(filename).withEnvironmentVariables().withSystemProperties().load()</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code class="inline language-java">loadProperties(properties, false)</code></td>
        <td><code class="inline language-java">builder().withProperties(properties).withEnvironmentVariables().withSystemProperties().load()</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code class="inline language-java">loadProperties(inputStream, false)</code></td>
        <td><code class="inline language-java">builder().withInputStream(inputStream).withEnvironmentVariables().withSystemProperties().load()</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Several calls with <code class="inline language-java">addProperties=true</code></td>
        <td>Add each source to one loader in merge order, then call <code class="inline language-java">load()</code> once.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Add values to settings you already loaded</td>
        <td><code class="inline language-java">builder().withConfig(previous).withProperties(overrides).load()</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The returned <code class="inline language-java">Map&lt;Property, Object&gt;</code></td>
        <td>Keep the returned <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig</code>; use <code class="inline language-java">config.asMap()</code> only for inspection.</td>
      </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig config = ConfigLoader.builder()
        .withProperties("base", baseProperties)
        .withProperties("deployment overrides", overrideProperties)
        .withEnvironmentVariables()
        .withSystemProperties()
        .load();</code></pre>

    <h3>Old read calls</h3>
    <p class="wide">
      Select the snapshot that replaces your old process-wide configuration, then use a schema-bound key for ordinary reads.
      The following examples assume the released call requested the property's actual value type; conversion cases need the extra review below.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Removed static call</th><th>Target call for a matching key</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>ConfigLoader.hasProperty(Property.SMTP_PORT)</code></td><td><code>config.hasProperty(ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ConfigLoader.getProperty(Property.SMTP_PORT)</code></td><td><code>config.get(ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT)</code>, with an Integer target</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ConfigLoader.getStringProperty(Property.SMTP_HOST)</code></td><td><code>config.get(ConfigKeys.SMTP_HOST)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ConfigLoader.getIntegerProperty(Property.SMTP_PORT)</code></td><td><code>config.get(ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ConfigLoader.getBooleanProperty(Property.JAVAXMAIL_DEBUG)</code></td><td><code>config.get(ConfigKeys.JAVAXMAIL_DEBUG)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ConfigLoader.valueOrProperty(port, Property.SMTP_PORT)</code></td><td><code>config.valueOrProperty(port, ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ConfigLoader.valueOrProperty(port, Property.SMTP_PORT, 25)</code></td><td><code>config.valueOrProperty(port, ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT, 25)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ConfigLoader.valueOrPropertyAsString/AsBoolean/AsInteger(...)</code></td><td>Use a matching typed key only when the old conversion is unnecessary; otherwise preserve the reviewed conversion explicitly.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="wide">
      Released scalar getters performed conversions. For example, <code>ConfigLoader.getStringProperty(Property.SMTP_PORT)</code>
      could return numeric port text. The key-based port read returns Integer. Preserve String/null behavior explicitly if the application needs that boundary:
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">Integer configuredPort = config.get(ConfigKeys.SMTP_PORT);
String portText = configuredPort == null ? null : configuredPort.toString();</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      Review dynamic Property arguments, explicit/fallback conversions, method references and numeric timeout consumers instead of applying
      a blanket method rename. The existing <code>Property</code> enum and <code>config.asMap().get(property)</code> support explicit raw integration,
      with Object values rather than a caller-inferred type. Time-valued Java reads use Duration while numeric property-file inputs keep their old units.
      See the <a href="configuration.html#section-typed-config-keys">normal configuration guide</a> for the final read contract.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      <code class="inline language-java">config.asMap()</code> is read-only. Unlike the map returned in 9.x, it will not change underneath you when
      somebody loads another set of properties later. Code that mutated the old map must instead load a new configuration with the intended values.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#config-behavior" id="config-behavior" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Check when settings are read and which sources are included</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>What stayed the same:</strong> the usual priority is still system properties over environment variables over
      <code class="inline language-text">simplejavamail.properties</code>. Version 10.0.0 deliberately keeps that 9.x behavior.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      For the usual setup, this is all you need:
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      What changed is how long those settings live. In 9.x, <code class="inline language-java">ConfigLoader</code> held one process-wide set of settings,
      and another <code class="inline language-java">loadProperties(...)</code> call could change what builders created later would see. In 10.0.0,
      <code class="inline language-java">fromDefaults()</code> reads the three conventional sources on its first call and keeps that result. Changing the
      file, an environment variable, or a system property later will not affect it. If your application reloads mail settings, create a new config and a
      new <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code> object explicitly. Also note that
      <code class="inline language-java">fromDefaults()</code> never reads Spring's Environment. The Spring module has its own setup, covered below.
    </p>

    <h3>If you previously called loadProperties</h3>
    <p class="wide">
      In 9.x, every <code class="inline language-java">loadProperties(...)</code> call also read environment variables and system properties, even when
      you only passed a Properties object, file, or stream. In 10.0.0, <code class="inline language-java">ConfigLoader.builder()</code> starts empty and
      only uses the sources you add. The explicit version of the usual setup is:
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig config = ConfigLoader.builder()
        .withClasspathResource("simplejavamail.properties")
        .withEnvironmentVariables()
        .withSystemProperties()
        .load();

SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(config);</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      Add those sources from lowest to highest priority. This is the direct replacement for repeated calls with
      <code class="inline language-java">addProperties=true</code>. Input streams are still read and closed as soon as you pass them in. Properties,
      maps, and custom sources are read when you call <code class="inline language-java">load()</code>. See the
      <a href="configuration.html#section-config-properties">configuration guide</a> for the complete ordering, blank-value, validation, and parsing
      rules.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#config-types" id="config-types" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Check types if you read config values yourself</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>You can skip this section</strong> if you never called <code class="inline language-java">ConfigLoader.getProperty(...)</code> yourself.
      The builders already ask for the right type.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      In 9.x, the loader tried to turn every piece of text into a boolean, integer, or known enum before it knew what the setting meant. That could produce
      some odd results: a host name equal to <code class="inline language-text">true</code> became a Boolean, and a numeric password became an Integer.
      Version 10.0.0 knows the expected type for each setting. Text settings now stay Strings, even when the text looks like another type.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
      <tr><th>Kind of setting</th><th>Value you get</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Host names, usernames, passwords, paths, algorithms, and other text</td><td><code class="inline language-java">String</code></td><td>Text such as <code class="inline language-text">true</code> or <code class="inline language-text">25</code> remains text.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>True/false settings</td><td><code class="inline language-java">Boolean</code></td><td>Text sources accept <code class="inline language-text">true/false</code>, <code class="inline language-text">yes/no</code>, and <code class="inline language-text">1/0</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ports and pool sizes</td><td><code class="inline language-java">Integer</code></td><td>A malformed winning value fails with the property key and source name.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Time settings read through ConfigKeys</td><td><code>Duration</code></td><td>Legacy numeric-millisecond inputs retain signed Integer limits and whole-millisecond precision; ISO-duration settings keep their own precision.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Transport, debug output, encoding, DSN return, pool balancing, and DKIM canonicalization choices</td><td>The matching enum</td><td>A map may contain either the enum value or its supported String form.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Extra Jakarta Mail properties and connection-pool clusters</td><td>Read-only maps</td><td>Entries under the wildcard property prefixes are combined by their complete key.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="wide">
      For dynamic map inputs, retain the parser representation: legacy numeric time settings still take Integer milliseconds or integer text, even though their key-based Java read returns Duration. Other values must match their schema input type. A wrong type now fails while loading the config. The error tells
      you which key and source caused it, but leaves out the actual value so credentials and private key data do not end up in logs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#config-lifecycle" id="config-lifecycle" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Swap mail settings without changing objects already in use</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>The rule is simple:</strong> new settings create new objects. They never rewrite a
      <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code> object, builder, Mailer, or Jakarta Mail Session that you already have.
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig mainConfig = ConfigLoader.builder()
        .withClasspathResource("main-mail.properties")
        .load();
SimpleJavaMail mainMail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(mainConfig);

SimpleJavaMailConfig backupConfig = ConfigLoader.builder()
        .withClasspathResource("backup-mail.properties")
        .load();
SimpleJavaMail backupMail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(backupConfig);</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      Both mail setups can now live in the same application without changing each other. This is also how to switch settings safely: create the new config,
      <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code> object, and Mailer first. Send new work through the replacement, let current work finish on
      the old Mailer, then close the old one. A builder you already created keeps the settings it started with.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      There is one conversion detail worth knowing. Static <code class="inline language-java">EmailConverter</code> methods that create an email builder
      use the settings from <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults()</code>. That includes MIME, EML, and Outlook conversion. If
      the converted email should use one of your explicitly loaded mail setups, start the conversion with
      <code class="inline language-java">mail.converter()</code> instead.
    </p>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java">// Uses the shared settings from SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults()
Email convertedWithDefaults = EmailConverter
        .mimeMessageToEmailBuilder(mimeMessage)
        .buildEmail();</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-java">// Uses main-mail.properties from mainMail above
Email convertedWithMainMail = mainMail.converter()
        .fromMimeMessage(mimeMessage)
        .toEmail();</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#submission-results" id="submission-results" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Check exception reporting and SMTP acceptance checks</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>What changes:</strong> SMTP submission failures now use the public
      <code class="inline language-java">MailSubmissionException</code> instead of an internal exception wrapper. Existing handlers that catch
      <code class="inline language-java">org.simplejavamail.MailException</code> still catch these failures. Update tests or reporting that depend on
      the concrete exception class. The original Jakarta Mail failure remains the direct cause, including for partially accepted submissions;
      those still fail the overall call.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      <code>EmailTooBigException</code> now extends <code>MailException</code> and reaches the caller directly instead of being hidden as a wrapper's
      cause. Catch it before <code>MailException</code> when size needs special handling. The released batch exception
      <code>BatchTransportException</code> also joins that root. A multi-catch combining either subtype with <code>MailException</code> is now invalid;
      use separate ordered catches or catch the common root. Review handlers that depended on the former wrapper or cause chain.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">Execution admission rejection is now <code>MailSendRejectedException</code>, also in the <code>MailException</code> family,
      including rejection by a borrowed executor. Move any special scheduling handler from <code>RejectedExecutionException</code> to that type;
      its cause retains the borrowed executor's original rejection. Keep ordinary application-executor errors outside this SJM-specific migration.</p>
    <p class="wide">Application exceptions are also more visible: RuntimeExceptions from your <code>CustomMailer</code> or lazy batch iterable
      propagate as those original exceptions, rather than being hidden inside the released sending wrapper. Review <code>catch (MailException ...)</code>
      blocks that previously doubled as handlers for your integration's own failures. Handle those application exceptions explicitly; invalid arguments,
      programming errors and JVM Errors are outside the common library-failure promise.</p>
    <p class="wide">
      <code class="inline language-java">MailSubmissionReceipt.isAcceptedByServer()</code> has been removed. In released 9.x it tested the captured
      SMTP response for a positive completion reply. The new predicates express submission status, so choose according to the caller's intent:
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java" id="example-acceptance-migration">boolean anyRecipientAccepted = receipt.isAcceptedForAtLeastOneRecipient();
boolean allRecipientsAccepted = receipt.isFullyAcceptedForSubmission();

// Retain the old 9.x positive-response test when that is what the application needs:
boolean positiveReply = receipt.getSmtpResponse()
    .map(SmtpServerResponse::isPositiveCompletionReply)
    .orElse(false);</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">The any-recipient predicate is true for partial acceptance even though that send fails. The full predicate is true only for
      <code>ACCEPTED</code>. Both are false for <code>UNKNOWN</code>, which is not proof of rejection. Full submission acceptance can remain true
      when later cleanup fails, and it does not establish final delivery. Do not mechanically rename a released 9.x reply test into either predicate.</p>
    <p class="wide">
      With Angus, <code class="inline language-properties">mail.smtp.reportsuccess=true</code> no longer makes a fully accepted transaction fail.
      Simple Java Mail collects success-reporting exceptions internally and returns an accepted receipt. Genuine failures still retain their original
      provider exception. Missing SMTP replies are absent rather than represented by code zero or an EOF sentinel; the original failure remains
      available for provider-specific inspection.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      See <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html">Analyzing send results</a> for the full receipt API and retry guidance.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="email-convenience-mailboxes">Check address-list interpretation and display names</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>From and bounce remain single-mailbox choices. Their String conveniences now reject successfully parsed lists and RFC groups rather
      than silently selecting their first mailbox. Nonempty malformed text remains available to your configured send-time validator.</p>
    <p>Reply-To and notification conveniences now parse every mailbox in a supplied list. Existing 9.x String calls could keep only the first
      parsed mailbox, so review list-valued and dynamic inputs. Repeated Reply-To calls remain additive; notification setters replace their whole
      local destination choice. The normal To/Cc/Bcc conveniences also accept lists; see <a href="features.html#section-recipient-builders">recipient examples</a>.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-java">Email email = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .from("sender@example.org")
    .to("customer@example.org")
    .withReplyTo("Support &lt;support@example.org&gt;, Accounts &lt;accounts@example.org&gt;")
    .withPlainText("Hello")
    .buildEmail();</code></pre>
    <p>A nonblank explicit name overrides every parsed mailbox name; null/blank retains each embedded name, including equivalent
      InternetAddress inputs. Quoted punctuation, comma/semicolon separators, order and duplicates remain intact. Use the plural recipient builder
      for a default-name policy. Prepared Recipient values are not reparsed. The plural recipient builder's String collection/varargs entries
      may each be complete lists; String and InternetAddress groups now consistently expand to their members.</p>
      <p><code>recipientBuilder().withAddress(String)</code> now also extracts the mailbox and embedded name from a single parseable address.
        A separately supplied name wins in either setter order; <code>withName(null)</code> restores the embedded name.
        Groups, multiple-mailbox strings and unparseable input remain intact for validation. The explicit address-only and
        default/fixed-name extraction helpers now retain such source too, rather than silently selecting only its first mailbox.
        Use <code>recipientsBuilder()</code> when every mailbox should become a recipient. The address-only helpers still preserve the configured
        name; the name-aware helpers still apply their explicit/default-name rule.</p>
    </div></section>

  <h2 id="notification-destination-lists">Read notification destinations as lists</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Replace Email and EmailPopulatingBuilder's <code>getDispositionNotificationTo()</code> and <code>getReturnReceiptTo()</code> calls with
      <code>getDispositionNotificationToRecipients()</code> and <code>getReturnReceiptToRecipients()</code>. These return immutable, non-null lists;
      absent or deferred destinations have an empty list. Decide whether your code needs to inspect every destination, check emptiness, or establish
      its own single-destination requirement; automatically taking element zero can hide additional mailboxes.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-java">for (Recipient destination : email.getDispositionNotificationToRecipients()) {
    System.out.println(destination.getAddress());
}</code></pre>
    <p>A no-argument notification request now uses every resolved Reply-To mailbox, falling back to the single From address. Explicit lists replace
      the local choice and resolve as one value through overrides/submitted/defaults. Request flags, clearing and suppression remain independent;
      an empty destination list alone does not mean the request is disabled. Notification destinations are not SMTP delivery recipients.</p>
    <p>Supported older serialized Emails remain readable: stored scalar destinations become immutable singleton lists, with their request flags
      preserved. This does not make new multi-destination snapshots compatible with older readers.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="smtp-empty-credentials">Review empty SMTP credential strings</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Empty username/password strings now remove their local credential values consistently through the individual setters and convenience
      overloads. The four-argument SMTP overload already normalized them in 9.3.6; individual setters and the three-argument overload did not.
      Review dynamically supplied empty values when upgrading. Whitespace is not trimmed, and clearing does not restore captured properties.</p>
    <p>Each overload still changes only its accepted fields: the three-argument overload retains the password, endpoint-only changes retain both
      credentials, and token providers remain separate. No overload is removed. Configuration locks continue to apply.</p>
  </div></section>
  <a href="#selected-mailer-templates" id="selected-mailer-templates" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Check templates when using several Mailers in a cluster</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>When a clustered send selects B's SMTP connection, it now uses B's Email defaults, overrides and validation settings too.
      Releases since 8.0.0 instead applied the Mailer you called, A, even if B actually sent the email. This could put A's relay-specific header,
      sender or BCC recipients on mail submitted through B.</p>
    <p>Review every registered member's templates. Put common requirements on each member and relay-specific values on the member that needs them.
      B is prepared from the original Email, not from an intermediate copy containing A's ordinary template. The correction also applies without
      a failover relationship. See the <a href="/smtp-connection-failover.html#section-ownership">ownership matrix</a>.</p>
  </div></section>
  <h2 id="copied-template-suppression">Check copies of emails that skip defaults or overrides</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>If an Email opts out of some defaults or overrides, copying it now keeps those choices. Earlier releases forgot them during
      <code>emailBuilder().copying(email)</code>, so a copied draft could receive values the original draft had excluded.</p>
    <p>Review code that depended on copying to restore templates. Edit the copy's <code>EmailTemplateOptions</code> and re-enable global inheritance with <code>ignoringDefaults(false)</code> and
      <code>ignoringOverrides(false)</code>. Call <code>clearDefaultValueSuppressions()</code> or <code>clearOverrideValueSuppressions()</code>
      to clear the corresponding per-field exclusions, then pass the edited value to <code>withTemplateOptions(...)</code>. Keep the inherited exclusions when the copy should retain
      the original Email's choices. These controls still cannot remove locked settings.</p>
    <p>Repeated <code>dontApplyDefaultValueFor(...)</code> and <code>dontApplyOverrideValueFor(...)</code> calls now accumulate exclusions;
      earlier calls replaced the entire set. Empty calls are now no-ops. For replacement, clear first and then supply the desired exclusions.
      Use <code>allowDefaultValueFor(...)</code> and <code>allowOverrideValueFor(...)</code> to remove individual exclusions.</p>
    <p><code>EmailProperty.ALL_RECIPIENTS</code> now excludes To, Cc and Bcc template additions together; earlier releases ignored that selector.
      If you used it but still relied on a template supplying recipients, remove the exclusion or select only the recipient types you want to skip.
      Your explicitly supplied recipients and the separately controlled delivery-recipient override are unchanged.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="typed-delivery-validation">Check typed delivery override addresses</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Addresses supplied through <code>withOverrideReceivers(...)</code> now receive the selected Mailer's configured address validation,
      encoded-word protection and injection checks after defaults and overrides are applied. Earlier releases skipped these destinations.
      Review routing addresses and configured validators when upgrading; recipient order, duplicate occurrences and metadata remain unchanged.</p>
    <p>Raw custom <code>To</code>, <code>Cc</code> and <code>Bcc</code> headers retain their advanced mailbox-validation escape, with general
      injection checks and mandatory locks. Warnings-only mode and exact EML's separate envelope contract keep their existing behavior.
      See <a href="features.html#section-email-validation">validation and rehearsal</a> and
      <a href="debugging.html#section-override-receivers">test-inbox routing</a>.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="calendar-template-values">Keep a template's calendar method and text together</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Calendar method and text now resolve together from one source: override, your Email, then default. Earlier releases resolved the two
      fields independently. Excluding <code>CALENDAR_METHOD</code> could leave inherited text without its required method and fail during
      completion; excluding either field from an override could combine its remaining component with your Email's or defaults' calendar.</p>
    <p>Either <code>CALENDAR_METHOD</code> or <code>CALENDAR_TEXT</code> now excludes that source's whole calendar contribution. Your Email's own
      calendar remains. Default exclusions still leave overrides eligible, and override exclusions leave your own or default calendar eligible.
      If you depended on mixed components, supply the intended complete calendar through <code>withCalendarText(method, text)</code>.</p>
    <p>Both arguments are required. A null method or text is now rejected before replacing an earlier calendar choice. Use
      <code>clearCalendarText()</code> for removal; non-null text remains unparsed, including empty text.</p>
    <p>Stored exclusions remain exactly as supplied. Allowing one selector leaves the calendar excluded when the other is still excluded;
      remove both exclusions to re-enable that source. Calendar transfer encoding remains independent, and locks retain their existing meaning.
      Exact EML sending, rehearsal and export keep their byte-preservation boundary; explicitly completing an exact Email still creates composed
      output. See <a href="configuration.html#section-config-mailer">defaults and overrides</a>.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="repeated-email-completion">Check repeated template collection additions</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Defaults and overrides now add only missing recipients, Reply-To destinations, delivery-recipient overrides, attachments and embedded
      images. Earlier releases appended every template entry, including values already present after
      <code>buildEmailCompletedWithDefaultsAndOverrides(...)</code>. Repeated completion or sending that result could therefore duplicate entries.</p>
    <p>If you intentionally relied on a template to repeat an existing recipient or resource, supply those occurrences directly on the Email.
      Submitted duplicates remain intact. Recipients match by their complete value, including role and metadata. Resources require matching
      metadata and recognized source identity; separate opaque sources are retained, and duplicate detection does not read their content.</p>
    <p>A completed Email still receives the selected Mailer's governance when sent: defaults fill missing scalar values, overrides retain
      precedence, and locks remain binding. Completion now also retains global and per-field suppression for later passes. Re-enable those
      choices explicitly if you previously relied on completion forgetting them. See the
      <a href="understanding-configuration.html#repeated-email-completion">complete repeated-completion contract</a>.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="forwarded-templates">Check forwarded messages in Email templates</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>If an Email defaults or overrides template contains a message to forward, that message now participates in preparation. Previously the
      template's forwarded message was ignored even though its other fields, such as the subject, could apply. A default fills a missing source;
      an override replaces the source supplied on the Email.</p>
    <p>If you depended on the source being ignored, remove it from the template or use
      <code>dontApplyDefaultValueFor(EmailProperty.MAIL_TO_FORWARD)</code> or
      <code>dontApplyOverrideValueFor(EmailProperty.MAIL_TO_FORWARD)</code> on that Email's <code>EmailTemplateOptions.Builder</code>. These exclusions preserve a source explicitly supplied on
      the Email. Subject selection remains independent, and exact EML submission still bypasses ordinary templates.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="copied-receipt-requests">Check receipt requests on copied emails</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Copying an Email now keeps requests for read or return receipts even when you let Simple Java Mail choose their return address.
      Earlier releases lost those address-free requests. Copies also keep an explicit choice to disable a request, so ordinary defaults
      cannot silently turn it back on.</p>
    <p>If your code relied on copying to remove a request, call <code>clearDispositionNotificationTo()</code> or
      <code>clearReturnReceiptTo()</code> on the copy instead. These methods restore unset request flags and addresses, so Email defaults or overrides
      may request a receipt again. To exclude inherited requests, use the existing template suppression for
      <code>USE_DISPOSITION_NOTIFICATION_TO</code> and <code>USE_RETURN_RECEIPT_TO</code>.
      There are no dedicated property keys for these requests; configured From/Reply-To addresses can supply an automatic destination.
      These message-header receipt requests are separate from SMTP delivery-status notifications.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="copied-delivery-overrides">Keep test-inbox routing when copying emails</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>If an Email uses <code>withOverrideReceivers(...)</code> to deliver to a test inbox, copying it now keeps that routing.
      Earlier releases lost the override when copying, so the copy could be sent to the real To/Cc/Bcc recipients instead.</p>
    <p>Both copying an Email and copying its builder now retain the override list, including its order and repeated recipients.
      If your application intentionally relied on a copy to restore ordinary delivery, call <code>clearOverrideReceivers()</code> on the copy's
      builder before <code>buildEmail()</code>. Configured defaults, overrides and locks still apply. The visible headers remain separate from the
      delivery envelope.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="imported-inline-resources">Check imported image lists</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>A referenced image without a disposition inside <code>multipart/related</code> now appears once in <code>Email.getEmbeddedImages()</code>.
      Earlier imports also placed that same resource in <code>getAttachments()</code>, so re-export could emit it twice. Remove any workaround
      that depends on that duplicate attachment entry. Explicitly attached, referenced images keep their downloadable and embedded views,
      and intentional resource occurrences remain unchanged.</p>
    <p>Valid external comments and folding whitespace on a received Content-ID no longer prevent the HTML CID URL from selecting its image.
      For example, <code>Content-ID: &lt;logo@example.org&gt; (company logo)</code> still identifies <code>cid:logo@example.org</code>.
      Earlier imports could expose that image as an ordinary attachment. Original HTML, image content and exact-source bytes remain unchanged.</p>
      <p>Imported attachment and embedded-image filenames now preserve literal angle brackets: <code>&lt;draft&gt;.txt</code> stays <code>&lt;draft&gt;.txt</code>. A supplied filename of <code>UnknownAttachment</code> also stays literal instead of being replaced by its Content-ID. Earlier versions could silently alter these names. Applications that matched the altered names should use the decoded source filename. Content-ID parsing and missing-filename fallbacks retain their existing behavior.</p>
    <p>Unreferenced inline parts with a repeated Content-ID now all survive as attachments in source order. Earlier imports retained only the
      last such part. Keep each returned occurrence when processing imported attachments; equal Content-IDs alone do not establish equal content.
      This correction preserves the existing selection when HTML references a duplicated ID.</p>
    <p>Percent-escaped CID references now identify their original image, for example <code>cid:logo%2F1@example.org</code> and
      <code>Content-ID: &lt;logo/1@example.org&gt;</code>. Such an image remains embedded rather than being treated as unreferenced.
      HTML attribute character references are recognized too: <code>src="cid:logo&amp;#64;example.org"</code> identifies
      <code>Content-ID: &lt;logo@example.org&gt;</code>. In <code>srcset</code>, each URL is interpreted separately from its descriptor, so
      <code>srcset="cid:logo&amp;#64;example.org 1x"</code> retains that image as embedded. Existing HTML, Content-IDs and bytes need no edits.
      See <a href="features.html#imported-inline-resources">conversion and inline resources</a>.</p>
    <p>Automatic image resolution now skips existing <code>cid:</code> schemes regardless of capitalization. An existing
      <code>CID:logo</code> reference no longer fails required external resolution; the Content-ID and HTML spelling remain unchanged.
      See <a href="features.html#automatic-image-cid-schemes">existing CID references</a>.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="attachment-reader-ownership">Handle attachment reads and stream ownership explicitly</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p><code>AttachmentResource.readAllBytes()</code> and <code>readAllData(...)</code> now close the input stream they open, even if reading fails.
      Previously those hidden streams stayed open. If your DataSource returns a shared stream, provide a fresh stream for each read instead;
      this also lets the same attachment be read again when sending.</p>
    <p>Opening, reading and closing I/O failures from these convenience methods now use <code>MailException</code> with kind
      <code>PREPARATION</code>. The original <code>IOException</code> remains the cause; if reading and closing both fail, the read error
      keeps the close error suppressed. The methods no longer declare checked <code>IOException</code>.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-java">try {
    byte[] content = attachment.readAllBytes();
} catch (MailException failure) {
    System.err.println("Cannot read attachment: " + failure.getCause());
}</code></pre>
    <p>Update handlers for these convenience calls. An <code>IOException</code> catch may become unreachable if the try block contains no
      other checked I/O; retain it when other operations still require it. Review multi-catches, method references and custom subclasses
      instead of replacing every I/O handler mechanically.</p>
    <p>Replace <code>getDataSourceInputStream()</code> with <code>openStream() throws IOException</code>. This method returns a stream for
      your code to consume and close; opening and subsequent stream operations use ordinary checked I/O failures.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-java">try (InputStream input = attachment.openStream()) {
    input.transferTo(destination);
}</code></pre>
    <p>Handle or declare <code>IOException</code> for this streaming code. The attachment's DataSource itself is not closed, and construction
      remains lazy. See <a href="features.html#section-reading-attachments">reading attachment content</a> for the two ownership choices.</p>
    <p>Eager MIME import also closes the attachment and calendar streams it acquires, including on read failure. This corrects the released
      static conversion routes as well as the current fluent route. Return a fresh stream from each DataSource read; a closing failure can now
      fail conversion. Lazy extraction and the existing ownership of caller-supplied top-level EML streams are unchanged.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="pool-setting-validation">Replace negative pool settings</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>When connection pooling is used, negative pool sizes and timeouts are now rejected consistently instead of being accepted on some configuration paths or failing later
      inside the pool. The batch module checks the effective settings before creating pool resources. Without that module, pool settings are unused.
      Use a core size of <code>0</code> to stop keeping a minimum number of connections open, a claim timeout of <code>0</code> for an immediate claim,
      or an idle expiry of <code>0</code> to disable idle expiration.</p>
    <p>The maximum size must be at least <code>1</code>, and the effective core size cannot exceed it after cluster overrides are applied.
      A configured creation-age expiry must remain positive; clear that setting to disable it.</p>
    <p>Use one property alias per cluster UUID. Earlier configurations could use a named alias and a direct UUID alias for the same cluster,
      silently discarding one partial configuration. Loading now rejects that ambiguity. Consolidate the leaves under one alias, retaining
      their ordinary or locked namespace; settings repeated under that same alias still use normal source precedence.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="dkim-validation-timing">Handle invalid DKIM header exclusions when signing</h2>
    <p>The 10.0.0 signing path now delegates to the shared upstream DKIM engine, including correct Ed25519-SHA256 signing.
      This correction preserves <code>signWithDomainKey(DkimConfig)</code>, the existing configuration keys and SJM's relaxed/relaxed defaults;
      it adds no caller migration step. The maintained 9.x line already uses the corrected upstream signing implementation.</p>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>If your DKIM configuration excludes the <code>From</code> header, that remains an error: a valid DKIM signature must cover it.
      Previously, creating the configuration failed immediately. The check now runs in the DKIM module when a message is signed,
      during conversion, rehearsal or sending. Configuration objects only retain the supplied choices.</p>
    <p>Remove <code>From</code> from the exclusion list. If your code catches configuration errors only around the builder, also handle
      signing failures at the operation that uses it. An asynchronous send reports the failure through its completion handle.</p>
    <p>DKIM also accepts equivalent capitalization between the sender domain and signing domain, including allowed subdomains.
      For example, <code>Sender@EXAMPLE.COM</code> can sign with <code>example.com</code>. Keep your existing address and domain
      configuration; their spelling is preserved, and unrelated domains and the usual DNS key requirements remain unchanged.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="message-snapshots">Stop changing emails through retained lists or arrays</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Building an Email now keeps its recipients, headers and other message metadata separate from the builder. Changing that builder later no
      longer changes an Email you already built. Stored message collections, including nested header values, cannot be changed; filtered To/Cc/Bcc
      lists and collection getters on builders return separate, editable copies. Recipient groups also copy their input list, and byte-array
      attachment/image input is copied.</p>
    <p>If your code used a returned collection or retained input array to change an existing message, make the change through a builder and build
      a replacement instead. A collection change can now throw <code>UnsupportedOperationException</code>, or affect only your detached copy.</p>
    <p>This does not freeze arbitrary attachment sources or forwarded <code>MimeMessage</code> objects. Keep those borrowed resources available
      and stable while sending, converting or serializing the Email.</p>
    <p>A failed required automatic-image resolution pass also leaves the builder's previous HTML and images intact. You can correct the HTML
      or supply the missing source and build again without keeping unused images from the failed pass. Explicit images and intentional duplicates
      remain unchanged. See the <a href="features.html#automatic-image-resolution-failure">automatic-image failure boundary</a>;
      this does not promise rollback for external reads or failures after resolution.</p>
    <p>Automatic image resolution also retains the opposite quote inside a quoted source: <code>&lt;img src="it's.png"&gt;</code>
      resolves <code>it's.png</code> rather than the prefix <code>it</code>. Existing templates need no edits. Code that accidentally relied on a
      truncated location now selects the full requested resource or follows the configured missing-image behavior. See
      <a href="features.html#automatic-image-source-quotes">quoted image sources</a>.</p>
    <p>Automatic resolution selects the actual quoted <code>src</code> attribute even when an earlier quoted attribute contains
      source-like text or <code>&gt;</code>. For example, <code>&lt;img alt="example src='decoy.png'" src="real.png"&gt;</code>
      resolves <code>real.png</code> while preserving the alt text. Existing templates need no edits; a decoy location is no longer
      looked up or embedded. See <a href="features.html#automatic-image-source-attributes">image source attributes</a>.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="operational-snapshots">Configure Mailers through their builders</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Changing the properties returned by <code>mailerBuilder.getProperties()</code> or
      <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig().getProperties()</code> now changes only a separate inspection copy. Previously those containers shared
      mutable storage, so reusing a builder could also change an earlier Mailer's reported configuration.</p>
    <p>Before construction, use <code>withProperty(...)</code>, <code>withProperties(...)</code> or <code>clearProperties()</code>.
      If you deliberately need to change an existing Session, use <code>mailer.getSession().getProperties()</code>; its settings and lifetime remain
      your responsibility. The operational snapshot does not track those later Session changes.</p>
    <p>Trusted-host arrays and builder inspection lists are also copied. Use <code>TlsConfig.Builder.trustingSSLHosts(...)</code> to replace the group's choice
      rather than changing a retained array or a returned list.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="header-replacement">Check custom headers that differ only in capitalization</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p><code>X-Route</code> and <code>x-route</code> now identify the same custom header when composing an Email or applying its templates.
      A replacing <code>withHeader(..., true)</code> call and an overriding template replace the earlier collection of values regardless of spelling.
      Previously, different capitalization could leave both values in the message.</p>
    <p>If you intended multiple values, add them without the replacement flag. Review templates that depended on capitalization to keep a conflicting
      value. Exact EML submission retains its original bytes.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="empty-custom-headers">Null custom headers produce empty fields</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p><code>withHeader("X-Example", null)</code> now produces a present empty header during ordinary conversion, rehearsal and sending,
      just as <code>withHeader("X-Example", "")</code> does. Previously, null was accepted by the builder but could cause a
      <code>NullPointerException</code> during validation or rendering.</p>
    <p>No source change is required. Null does not mean omission: applications that want an absent field should leave it out of their header
      additions. Stored null metadata, copying and serialization remain unchanged. Existing empty imported headers remain supported,
      and exact/protected source bytes retain their preservation contract.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="recipient-equality">Review Email comparisons</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Email equality now counts repeated recipient entries correctly. An Email addressed to Alice and Bob does not equal one addressed to Alice twice;
      previously the answer could depend on which Email was on the left of <code>equals(...)</code>.</p>
    <p>Recipient order is still ignored for equality. Review any deduplication or collection lookup that relied on the incorrect comparison;
      sending continues to preserve recipient occurrences.</p>
    <p>Equality also distinguishes one attachment or embedded image from two occurrences of it, while still ignoring their order and comparing
      their metadata without reading content. DKIM configuration now participates too, as the other signing configurations already did:
      an unsigned Email no longer equals an otherwise identical DKIM-configured Email. Review equality-based caches or deduplication accordingly.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="eml-export-failures">Handle content failures during email conversion</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>If an attachment cannot be read or message content cannot be written when converting an Email to MIME or exporting MIME as EML, the operation
      now throws a conversion exception in the <code>MailException</code> family with an explanation and the original cause.
      Previously these failures threw <code>IllegalStateException</code>, sometimes saying <code>"This should never happen"</code>, even for an
      ordinary unavailable attachment.</p>
    <p>If your code specifically caught that <code>IllegalStateException</code>, catch <code>MailException</code> around the conversion instead.
      Keep borrowed attachment sources available until export completes.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="address-only-name">Check names when importing only a recipient address</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p><code>withAddressOnlyFrom("Parsed name &lt;alice@example.org&gt;")</code> now keeps the name already configured on the recipient builder,
      matching the overload that accepts an <code>InternetAddress</code>. Earlier releases unexpectedly replaced it with the parsed display name.</p>
    <p>If you relied on that replacement, set the desired name explicitly with <code>withName(...)</code>, or use
      <code>withAddressAndNameOrDefault(source, null)</code> when you want both the parsed name and address.</p>
    <p>The address-import helpers also keep nonempty text when Jakarta Mail parses it into no mailbox, for example <code>"(comment only)"</code>.
      Earlier releases could throw <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> during construction. Handle invalid addresses through your normal
      validation path; retaining the input does not allow it to pass sending validation.</p>
    <p>Rejected recipient shortcuts and address/name helpers now retain the earlier complete identity instead of combining a replacement name with
      the old address or dropping an inferred name. No calls need renaming. Separately accepted setters retain their effects when a later setter
      fails; see <a href="features.html#rejected-recipient-updates">recipient update failures</a>.</p>
  </div></section>

    <h2 id="requested-smime-module">Include S/MIME support when requesting decryption</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>If you supply decryption keys to an EML, MIME or Outlook conversion, Simple Java Mail now requires <code>smime-module</code> on the runtime
      classpath. Previously the request could be silently ignored when the module was missing.</p>
    <p>Include the module when you want decryption. If you only want ordinary inspection without decryption, omit the keys or clear the configured
      decryption request. Reading mail without an explicit decryption request still does not require S/MIME support.</p>
    <p>Ordinary imports of protected input without that module no longer report the default <code>PLAIN</code> S/MIME mode. Recognizable headers
      retain the outer <code>SIGNED</code> or <code>ENCRYPTED</code> mode; ambiguous S/MIME reports <code>UNKNOWN</code>. Update code that treats every
      mode other than the known protected modes as confirmed plain content. The inspection result getters expose <code>UNKNOWN</code> when
      verification or decryption could not be established, and the nullable signature-valid getter stays <code>null</code> in that case.
      Clearly plain input keeps its existing plain, unsigned and unencrypted results.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="retained-smime-signatures">Keep detached signatures in retained decrypted EML</h2>
    <section><div class="wide">
      <p>After decrypting an Outlook message containing a detached S/MIME signature, the generated <code>signed-email.eml</code> and
        retained signed-content view keep the signed MIME container and its signature material. Save the generated attachment's bytes
        when a later reader needs to verify that signature; readable-content merging remains a separate choice.</p>
      <p>This restores retained content without changing Java calls or configuration. Standard and legacy opaque signed-data handling,
        established decryption facts and ordinary content/resource ownership keep their existing behavior. See
        <a href="security.html#section-reading-smime-merging">retained S/MIME content and merging</a>.</p>
    </div></section>

  <h2 id="smime-media-type-capitalization">Keep equivalent S/MIME declarations intact</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>With S/MIME support installed, media-type capitalization no longer makes a signed or encrypted message appear plain.
      <code>Multipart/Signed</code> and <code>multipart/signed</code> select the same inspection. Remove workarounds that rewrite
      protected mail solely to normalize its type; pass the original source to conversion. The correction preserves original headers and bytes.</p>
    <p>The same applies to known <code>smime-type</code> values: <code>SIGNED-DATA</code> selects signature processing rather than decryption,
      and <code>ENVELOPED-DATA</code> reports encrypted content. Missing and unknown parameter values retain their existing interpretation.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="existing-mime-smime-protection">Review S/MIME protection of an existing MimeMessage</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p><code>MailerHelper.signAndOrEncryptMessageWithSmime(session, message, email)</code> keeps its signature and now honors independently
      selected signing and encryption, including recipient certificates with the Email-level fallback and algorithm choices. Earlier versions
      unconditionally required both configurations: encryption-only failed before encryption, while signing-only signed and then failed.</p>
    <p>Pass the MIME message you want protected and an Email containing your already selected S/MIME settings. Caller changes to the MIME content
      and headers remain the input; the helper does not rebuild the message, apply Mailer templates, or apply DKIM/OpenPGP. When both operations
      are selected, signing precedes encryption and each transformed stage is finalized. With neither selected, the same message is returned
      without reading its content or loading <code>smime-module</code>; requested protection still requires the module.</p>
    <p>No source change is required. Remove workarounds that supplied an unwanted second operation only if that matches your intended policy.
      See <a href="features.html#section-protect-existing-mime">protecting existing MIME content</a> for an encryption-only example.</p>
  </div></section>

  <h2 id="async-preparation">Account for validation running during async execution</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Asynchronous sends now apply the selected destination's templates and validate the Email when the executor runs the operation, not before
      queue admission on the caller. Observe the returned completion to handle these operational failures. Immediate argument errors, such as a
      null Email, still throw directly. Your executor may use caller-runs behavior, so this does not guarantee a different physical thread.</p>
    <p>If application code depended on caller-thread validation side effects or their ordering before admission, move that work explicitly or update
      it for executor execution. Synchronous sends still perform the work on the caller.</p>
  </div></section>
  <h2 id="api-construction">Use public contracts instead of constructing library results</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>If you created recipient builders directly, get them from your <code>SimpleJavaMail</code> factory instead.
      The concrete <code>org.simplejavamail.recipient.RecipientBuilder</code> and <code>RecipientsBuilder</code> classes are no longer application entry points.</p>
    <p>A prepared Recipient with no role now defaults to To when added as a visible message recipient. It is retained through copying and completion
      instead of being silently omitted. Supply Cc/Bcc explicitly when intended. The supplied value is unchanged, and From/Reply-To roles remain optional.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();
IRecipientBuilder recipient = mail.recipientBuilder();
IRecipientsBuilder recipients = mail.recipientsBuilder();

Recipient alice = recipient.to("Alice", "alice@example.org");</code></pre>
    <p>Import the builder interfaces from <code>org.simplejavamail.api.email</code>. Also update imports of the template-suppression selector
      from <code>org.simplejavamail.internal.config.EmailProperty</code> to <code>org.simplejavamail.api.email.EmailProperty</code>;
      the selected fields and suppression behavior are unchanged.</p>
    <p><code>MailSubmissionReceipt</code>, <code>OutlookMessageData</code> and <code>OutlookEmailConversionResult</code> are now read interfaces.
      Keep receiving and inspecting them through sending or conversion calls; remove direct constructor calls.
      Recompile integrations compiled against the old concrete classes. Adapter implementations return
      <code>MailTransportResult</code> rather than constructing an application receipt.</p>
    <p>Inside <code>withOpenConnection(...)</code>, use <code>sender.sendMail(email)</code> whether or not you need a receipt. It now returns
      <code>MailSubmissionReceipt</code>, just like the synchronous Mailer view. Replace calls to the removed
      <code>sender.sendMailAndGetReceipt(email)</code> with <code>sender.sendMail(email)</code>. Calls that ignore the result need no source change,
      but recompile existing integrations because the return type changed. Update wrappers or test doubles to return the real sender's receipt.</p>
    <p>Previously serialized Java objects containing <code>MailSubmissionReceipt</code> or <code>OutlookMessageData</code> cannot be read by
      the new implementations. Before upgrading, read those stored reports with the old library and export the observations your application needs.
      This does not remove compatibility for serialized <code>Email</code>, <code>Recipient</code> or attachment data.</p>
    <p>The two deprecated <code>EmailConverter.outlookMsgToEmailBuilder(InputStream, ...)</code> overloads now return
      <code>OutlookEmailConversionResult</code>, not the internal <code>EmailFromOutlookMessage</code> wrapper. Use
      <code>getEmailBuilder()</code> for the editable email and <code>getOutlookMessageData()</code> for the source metadata.
      New calls should use <code>outlookMsgToEmailBuilderWithOutlookData(...)</code>.</p>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#dkim-defaults" id="dkim-defaults" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Move DKIM defaults into Email templates</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      <strong>What breaks:</strong> If you configured a default DKIM signature on the Mailer, move those signing settings onto an Email and pass it to
      <code>withEmailDefaults(...)</code>, as shown below. That Email acts as a reusable template for messages you send through the Mailer.
      The dedicated methods <code>withDefaultDkimSigning(...)</code>, <code>clearDefaultDkimSigning()</code>,
      <code>getDefaultDkimSigningConfig()</code>, and <code>isDefaultDkimSigningConfigured()</code> are removed in 10.0.0.
      Use <code>withEmailOverrides(...)</code> instead when the template should replace a message's own signing choice.
      Recompile integrations using the removed methods.
    </p>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();
Email defaults = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .signWithDomainKey(dkimConfig)
    .buildEmail();
mailerBuilder.withEmailDefaults(defaults);</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="wide">
      Both former signing overloads exist on the Email builder. The template may omit the sender and recipients; the message you send
      still needs them. DKIM applies to the whole message, not individual recipients. Its precedence is the same as other single values:
      override, submitted Email, then default, subject to per-email opt-outs.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      A supplied defaults template replaces the property-derived template, rather than merging with it. That behavior is unchanged.
      To keep unrelated property defaults while changing or omitting the default signature, build an Email containing those defaults and edit a copy:
    </p>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java">Email configuredDefaults = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .buildEmailCompletedWithDefaultsAndOverrides();

// Keep other configured message defaults and replace the signature.
Email signingDefaults = mail.emailBuilder().copying(configuredDefaults)
    .signWithDomainKey(dkimConfig)
    .buildEmail();
mailerBuilder.withEmailDefaults(signingDefaults);

// Or omit only default DKIM signing.
Email unsignedDefaults = mail.emailBuilder().copying(configuredDefaults)
    .clearDkim()
    .buildEmail();
mailerBuilder.withEmailDefaults(unsignedDefaults);</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="wide">
      Building that completed Email reads configured key files and decodes inline key data before you can clear DKIM. Unlike the removed dedicated DKIM clear flag,
      this recipe cannot skip an unreadable key file or malformed Base64 setting. A replacement template built with ordinary <code>buildEmail()</code> skips
      property-derived message defaults entirely.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      On an Email you send, <code>clearDkim()</code> removes the signature configured on that Email and allows a default again. To prevent a default signature for
      that Email, use <code>dontApplyDefaultValueFor(EmailProperty.DKIM_SIGNING_CONFIG)</code>. Use
      <code>dontApplyOverrideValueFor(...)</code> separately to ignore an override. See
      <a href="configuration.html#section-config-mailer">the full defaults and overrides contract</a> for collection and recipient behavior.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      <code>useConfiguredEmailDefaults()</code> replaces the old <code>clearEmailDefaults()</code> name and restores property-derived defaults.
      <code>withoutEmailDefaults()</code> omits ordinary defaults while retaining locks. An empty explicit template also omits all
      default message settings. Inspect your explicit template through <code>mailerBuilder.getEmailDefaults()</code>, or the Mailer's effective defaults and overrides through
      <code>mailer.getEmailGovernance()</code>; there is no separate DKIM configured flag.
      DKIM property keys and Spring configuration are unchanged.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      CLI signing configuration continues to use <code>simplejavamail.dkim.signing.*</code> properties; Java signing objects are not CLI values.
      The generated <code>--mailer:clearDefaultDkimSigning</code> option is removed too. Omit the DKIM default from the configuration used by that
      invocation, or use <code>--email:ignoringDefaultsYesNo true</code> to skip all message defaults and supply the required values yourself.
      <code>--email:clearDkim</code> clears only the local choice, not incoming defaults.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#mailer-validation" id="mailer-validation" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Update assumptions about Mailer validation</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>What changes:</strong> <code class="inline language-java">mailer.validate(email)</code> now checks whether this Mailer can prepare the
      complete email it would send, including its defaults and overrides. In 9.x it only checked the Email you passed in. Validation can therefore
      fail on configured signing keys or the final email size even when that original Email is valid.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">Both <code>Mailer.validate</code> overloads and <code>MailerHelper.validate</code>/<code>validateLenient</code> now return
      <code>void</code>. Replace boolean assignments and conditions with direct calls. Strict validation still throws on failure;
      lenient helper validation still logs ordinary findings. A normal return never promised server acceptance.</p>
    <p class="wide">
      The default method now runs ordinary validation, builds the complete MIME message, applies configured signing and encryption, and checks the
      final encoded-size limit. This can read attachments and key files, so do not treat it as a cheap field-only check. It does not connect to SMTP
      or change the supplied Email.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      If you specifically need the old checks on the supplied Email without preparing a message, use
      <code class="inline language-java">MailerHelper.validate(email)</code>. To keep a custom validator, use its
      <code class="inline language-java">validate(email, emailValidator)</code> overload.
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">// Check only the Email supplied by your code, as in 9.x:
MailerHelper.validate(email);

// Check the complete message this Mailer would prepare:
mailer.validate(email);</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      Full Mailer validation now needs Angus Mail or another compatible Jakarta Mail implementation to build MIME. The CLI
      <code class="inline language-text">validate</code> command also performs complete preparation without connecting to SMTP.
      See <a href="features.html#section-email-validation">validation and rehearsal</a> for the current preparation APIs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#smtp-content-compatibility" id="smtp-content-compatibility" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Check sends with international addresses or unencoded non-ASCII text</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      If you send to an address such as <code class="inline language-text">josé@example.org</code>, or send a saved EML file with unencoded non-ASCII
      headers or body text, your SMTP server must announce support for that content by default. With the bundled Angus provider, Simple Java Mail now checks this
      before sending. Ordinary Unicode subjects, display names and bodies still use their usual email encoding, so most applications need no change.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      The server announces these capabilities as <code class="inline language-text">SMTPUTF8</code> and
      <code class="inline language-text">8BITMIME</code>. Some servers handle the content without announcing the relevant capability. A send that worked
      with such a server in 9.x can now fail before the SMTP <code class="inline language-text">MAIL FROM</code> command, with
      <code class="inline language-java">MailSubmissionException</code> and a
      <code class="inline language-java">MailTransportCompatibilityException</code> cause. Prefer a server advertising the needed capabilities.
      If you have verified that the existing server and onward route preserve the original content, explicitly retain that legacy behavior:
    </p>
<pre><code class="language-java">mailerBuilder.withConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig.builder()
    .withLegacySmtpContentSupport(true)
    .build());</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.smtp.legacycontentsupport=true</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      This option is disabled by default and applies to the server configuration actually selected for the send, including in a connection-pool cluster.
      Enabling it attempts the original content without adding SMTP parameters for capabilities the server did not announce. It does not guarantee that
      later servers will preserve or deliver that content, relax security checks, replace mailbox characters or automatically retry a rejected message.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Library-owned stock Angus Sessions now enable <code class="inline language-properties">mail.mime.allowutf8</code> before preparing MIME or creating
      transports. The managed transport declares SMTPUTF8 per message. Explicit false overrides and caller-owned Sessions retain their settings.
      If an internationalized send fails because UTF-8 output is disabled, remove that override or enable it before creating transports.
      Caller-owned ordinary Angus transports keep their Session-wide declaration behavior.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Fixtures using Angus's <code class="inline language-properties">mail.smtp.allow8bitmime</code> or
      <code class="inline language-properties">mail.smtps.allow8bitmime</code> should now expect
      <code class="inline language-text">BODY=8BITMIME</code> when conversion can produce raw 8-bit bytes and the server advertises support.
      An entirely ASCII body does not require the extension just because it is labelled 8bit.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Malformed UTF-8 headers, binary transfer encoding, NUL body bytes and body lines exceeding 998 bytes (excluding CRLF) now fail locally.
      Regenerate invalid source headers and use Base64 or quoted-printable for unsuitable bodies before signing or loading final bytes.
      The legacy setting does not bypass these checks. See the <a href="features.html#section-international-mail">international-mail workflow</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#smtp-size-check" id="smtp-size-check" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Account for rejection before sending oversized email</h2>
  <section>
    <p class="wide">If your SMTP server announces a maximum message size, Simple Java Mail now rejects larger emails before transmitting them over
      an Angus connection it manages. Previously, it could send the email and wait for the server to reject it. The check only uses a reliable positive
      limit. If your server used to accept mail above its own announced limit, correct that limit, reduce the email, or choose another server.
      A local rejection has no SMTP reply code; inspect the failure receipt instead.</p>
    <p class="wide">SMTP calls this capability <code>SIZE</code>. The client adds <code>SIZE=&lt;bytes&gt;</code> when the server announces support.
      If you supply this manually through
      <code>SMTPMessage.setMailExtension(...)</code> or <code>mail.smtp.mailextension</code> / <code>mail.smtps.mailextension</code>,
      remove the manual value to use automatic calculation. A single valid estimate is preserved, but malformed or duplicate declarations now fail locally.</p>
    <p class="wide">Counting adds a serialization pass, so caller-owned attachment and content sources must stay readable and repeatable.
      The server's limit is separate from your own <code>ValidationConfig.Builder.withMaximumEmailSize(...)</code> limit. Caller-owned ordinary Angus transports retain their behavior.
      See <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-message-size">message size diagnostics</a> for receipts and illustrative output.</p>
  </section>

  <a href="#opportunistic-tls" id="opportunistic-tls" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Configure optional SMTP encryption on the Mailer builder</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>What breaks:</strong> If you disabled automatic encryption upgrades with
      <code class="inline language-java">TransportStrategy.SMTP.setOpportunisticTLS(false)</code>, move that choice to
      <code class="inline language-java">withOpportunisticTLS(false)</code> on <code>TlsConfig.Builder</code>, supplied through <code>withTlsConfig(...)</code>. The old setter, which changed the shared default for
      new Mailers using <code class="inline language-java">SMTP</code>, has been removed. This option controls whether plain SMTP tries to upgrade to an
      encrypted connection when the server offers STARTTLS; it does not require encryption.
    </p>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java">// 9.x
TransportStrategy.SMTP.setOpportunisticTLS(false);
Mailer mailer = MailerBuilder
        .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP)
        .buildMailer();</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-java">// 10.0.0
Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
        .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP)
        .withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
            .withOpportunisticTLS(false)
            .build())
        .buildMailer();</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="wide">
      You probably never changed this option. It is an escape hatch for plain SMTP servers that break during STARTTLS negotiation, and it only affects
      <code class="inline language-java">TransportStrategy.SMTP</code>. It has no effect on mandatory
      <code class="inline language-java">SMTP_TLS</code>, OAuth2 transport, or implicit TLS with
      <code class="inline language-java">SMTPS</code>. The property <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.opportunistic.tls</code>
      still supplies the starting value. A conflicting builder call requires the deliberate override policy; strict mode rejects the disagreement.
      If you want the property or the normal default, do not call
      <code class="inline language-java">TlsConfig.Builder.withOpportunisticTLS(...)</code> at all.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      If you call <code class="inline language-java">TransportStrategy.SMTP.generateProperties()</code> yourself, it now always includes the properties
      for optional STARTTLS. Disabling optional STARTTLS only takes effect while a Mailer is being built.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#mandatory-starttls" id="mandatory-starttls" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Remove settings that contradict required SMTP encryption</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      <strong>What changes:</strong> Choosing <code class="inline language-java">SMTP_TLS</code> or
      <code class="inline language-java">SMTP_OAUTH2</code> asks for an encrypted SMTP connection. If you also supply the advanced setting
      <code class="inline language-properties">mail.smtp.starttls.required=false</code>, those choices contradict each other.
      Simple Java Mail now rejects that combination during <code class="inline language-java">buildMailer()</code>, before opening connections or
      starting a proxy or pool. Previously, it could send credentials without encryption when the server did not offer STARTTLS.
      Remove the conflicting setting; property-file, Spring and CLI values are checked too.
    </p>
<pre><code class="language-java">// Remove this contradictory override:
// .withProperty("mail.smtp.starttls.required", "false")

Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "username", "password")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      Remove <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.extraproperties.mail.smtp.starttls.required=false</code> from external
      configuration as well. If another configuration source supplies it, a final Java
      <code class="inline language-java">withProperty("mail.smtp.starttls.required", null)</code> removes the inherited override.
      Omit the property to use the strategy default, or use <code class="inline language-java">Boolean.TRUE</code> or a case-insensitive
      <code class="inline language-text">"true"</code> string without surrounding whitespace. Malformed values also fail construction;
      they do not mean true to the provider.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      If plaintext fallback is intentional for username/password authentication, choose
      <code class="inline language-java">TransportStrategy.SMTP</code>. The default remains opportunistic SMTP, including with credentials:
      try STARTTLS when offered and allow plaintext when it is absent. A failed upgrade or certificate check still fails the connection.
      OAuth2 keeps mandatory STARTTLS; do not switch access tokens to the ordinary password strategy as a workaround.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      The check also applies in logging-only mode, but not to caller-owned Sessions or a
      <code class="inline language-java">CustomMailer</code>. It checks this override at construction, not later Session mutations,
      arbitrary provider or socket-factory replacements, or certificate-trust exceptions. No default port or public signature changes.
      See <a href="security.html#section-authentication-methods">choosing SMTP encryption and authentication</a> and
      <a href="debugging.html#section-config-diagnostics">configuration provenance diagnostics</a> to trace a loaded override.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#custom-factory-fallback" id="custom-factory-fallback" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>If you supply your own SMTP connection code</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide"><strong>What changes:</strong> If your application supplies its own code for opening SMTP connections (a custom socket or SSL
      factory), a failure in that code now stops the connection attempt. Previously, Angus could try Java's built-in connection code instead,
      bypassing settings you chose, such as a client certificate or custom certificate checks. This changes the default for Sessions created by
      Simple Java Mail; an explicit fallback setting still takes precedence. If you haven't supplied a custom factory, no change is needed.</p>
    <p class="wide">If your deployment intentionally depended on that retry, retain it with the existing advanced property:
      <code>mailerBuilder.withProperty("mail.smtp.socketFactory.fallback", "true")</code>, or
      <code>simplejavamail.extraproperties.mail.smtp.socketFactory.fallback=true</code> in your properties file or Spring configuration.
      For SMTPS, replace <code>mail.smtp</code> with <code>mail.smtps</code> in either form.
      Only enable this if a connection made without your custom factory's settings is acceptable for your deployment.
      STARTTLS failure still does not fall back to plaintext. Caller-owned Sessions keep their existing behavior.</p>
    <p class="wide">Simple Java Mail puts a cleanup wrapper around SSL factories used by Sessions it creates. If you read the factory back from
      the Session properties, you therefore get that wrapper, not your original object. Keep your original reference if you need methods specific
      to your factory implementation.</p>
    <p class="wide">For a class-based SSL factory in a modular application, export its implementation package to
      <code>org.simplejavamail.mailprovider.angus</code>, or use <code>TlsConfig.Builder.withCustomSSLFactoryInstance(...)</code>.
      The cleanup wrapper calls its public static <code>getDefault()</code> method and follows the normal Java module-access rules.
      See <a href="security.html#section-custom-ssl-factory">custom SSL factory guidance</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <a href="#proxy-bridge-port" id="proxy-bridge-port" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Check fixed local ports when using SOCKS proxy authentication</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>What changes:</strong> If your SOCKS proxy requires a username and password, Simple Java Mail starts a local proxy bridge to handle that
      authentication. The bridge now chooses a free local port instead of always using <code class="inline language-text">1081</code>, so two Mailers
      can use it without competing for the same port. If you explicitly set a positive bridge port, it still stays fixed.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      You no longer have to give every authenticated-proxy Mailer its own bridge port. Separate Mailers can run at the same time without fighting over
      the old default. Simple Java Mail writes the selected port to the effective Session before the SMTP connection opens, and updates it if the bridge
      later restarts on another port. Anonymous SOCKS proxies are unchanged.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Remove <code class="inline language-java">withProxyBridgePort(...)</code> or
      <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.proxy.socks5bridge.port</code> when you only set it to avoid collisions. Setting the value
      to <code class="inline language-text">0</code> also selects automatic allocation. Keep a positive value only when the application really needs a
      predictable local port:
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
        .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
            .withHost("proxy.example.com")
            .withPort(1080)
            .withUsername("user")
            .withPassword("password")
            .withBridgePort(7777)
            .build())
        .buildMailer();</code></pre>
    <h3>Check proxy destinations and credential lengths</h3>
    <p class="wide">
      The authenticated bridge now carries IPv6 destinations and forwards names that the JVM could not resolve to the remote proxy.
      Its generated local hop uses IPv4 loopback <code>127.0.0.1</code> for provider compatibility, while destination and remote-proxy connections
      can still use IPv6. The provider/JDK's initial local lookup behavior remains unchanged.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      For an active authenticated route, each effective proxy username and password must contain 1 through 255 UTF-8 bytes.
      <code>buildMailer()</code> now rejects an invalid effective length before connecting instead of sending a malformed authentication request.
      Check credentials loaded from deployment configuration as well as Java choices; non-ASCII characters can occupy more than one byte.
      The Java builder calls and property names are unchanged.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      A proxy must also return a complete, valid CONNECT reply before SMTP forwarding starts. Truncated replies and invalid protocol fields now
      fail setup. See <a href="configuration.html#section-proxy-bridge-ports">authenticated proxy configuration</a> for the current boundaries.
    </p>
    <h3>Keep established tunnels open while new connections start</h3>
    <p class="wide">
      Earlier bridge implementations kept setup workers occupied for the lifetime of established tunnels. In the unreleased 10.0.0 line,
      forwarding releases that setup capacity, so pooled or retained connections can stay open while new connections start proxy setup.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Simultaneous unfinished setups remain bounded. When setup capacity is full, only the new setup is refused; healthy established tunnels
      remain usable. Idle setup workers can retire. Existing proxy settings and timeouts are unchanged.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#direct-session" id="direct-session" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>If you create Jakarta Mail Sessions yourself</h2>

  <section>
    <h3>Use one Mailer per Session</h3>
    <p class="wide">
      Earlier releases allowed two Mailers to use the same Jakarta Mail Session. Building the second could silently replace the first Mailer's
      email defaults, overrides, and connection configuration. In 10.0.0, the second build throws an exception before changing that configuration.
      This also applies to two different Sessions using the same <code class="inline language-java">Properties</code> object, and to a Session
      obtained from another Mailer's <code class="inline language-java">getSession()</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Reuse one Mailer when both callers should use the same configuration. It already supports concurrent sending:
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">try (Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder(session).buildMailer()) {
    mailer.sync().sendMail(firstEmail);
    mailer.async().sendMail(secondEmail).getCompletion().join();
}</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      If the callers need different Mailer configurations, create separate Sessions with separate Properties. Copy your original SMTP settings,
      not the properties of a Session already configured by a Mailer:
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">Properties firstProperties = new Properties();
firstProperties.putAll(smtpProperties);
Properties secondProperties = new Properties();
secondProperties.putAll(smtpProperties);

Session firstSession = Session.getInstance(firstProperties);
Session secondSession = Session.getInstance(secondProperties);

try (Mailer first = mail.mailerBuilder(firstSession).buildMailer();
     Mailer second = mail.mailerBuilder(secondSession).buildMailer()) {
    first.sync().sendMail(firstEmail);
    second.sync().sendMail(secondEmail);
}</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      Use <code class="inline language-java">Session.getInstance(...)</code> rather than
      <code class="inline language-java">Session.getDefaultInstance(...)</code> when creating independent Sessions; the latter can return an
      existing Session. You can reuse a Session after its previous Mailer has shut down successfully. Wait for
      <code class="inline language-java">close()</code> or the shutdown future to complete; starting shutdown does not release ownership.
      If cleanup fails, create a fresh Session instead.
    </p>
    <h3>Keep your Session's connection settings unless you explicitly replace them</h3>
    <p class="wide">
      Earlier releases treated supplied Sessions inconsistently. A Session originating from Simple Java Mail could have its timeouts and
      certificate-trust choices replaced by builder defaults, while another Session could silently ignore an explicit timeout setter.
      Both now use the supplied settings as the starting point. Ordinary factory connection defaults no longer replace them or fill omitted
      values. Deliberate builder customization and applicable locks can add settings; different explicit declarations fail under the default
      <a href="#connection-conflicts">connection conflict policy</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      If you relied on those automatic replacements, call the relevant builder setters explicitly or put the intended values on your Session.
      To deliberately replace existing values, select <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code>. With that policy,
      <code class="inline language-java">ConnectionConfig.Builder.withSessionTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(1))</code> replaces all three socket timeouts with one second.
      Selecting only <code>withReadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5))</code> changes the read value and retains the Session's original connect and write values or absence.
      Standard SMTP/SMTPS Sessions support these choices directly. A custom protocol now rejects a convenience setting it cannot map rather
      than silently ignoring it; use its provider-specific names through <code>withProperty(...)</code> instead.
      Clearing a setting inherited through <code>Properties</code> defaults is also rejected when removal would simply reactivate that default.
      Supply a replacement value or a fresh Session with independent Properties containing only the intended settings.
    </p>
    <h3 id="tls-identity-inspection">Handle an unspecified TLS identity setting</h3>
    <p class="wide">Replace <code>OperationalConfig.isVerifyingServerIdentity()</code>, which returned primitive <code>boolean</code>, with
      <code>OperationalConfig.getVerifyingServerIdentity()</code>, which returns nullable <code>Boolean</code>. Recompile integrations and update
      method references as well as direct calls. True and false mean known configured values; null means unspecified or unrecognized,
      including an omitted property on a supplied Session or an opaque protocol. It does not mean that its provider disables verification.
      Simple Java Mail leaves the omitted property absent and keeps the provider's runtime behavior.</p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">Boolean verification = mailer.getOperationalConfig().getVerifyingServerIdentity();
if (verification == null) {
    log.info("Server identity verification: unspecified");
} else if (verification) {
    log.info("Server identity verification: configured on");
} else {
    log.info("Server identity verification: configured off");
}</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">Choose how your application handles the unknown case explicitly. A direct rename in a primitive assignment or condition
      can throw when Java unboxes null. Using <code>Boolean.TRUE.equals(...)</code> also combines unspecified and false; use it only when that
      is your intended application policy. The getter captures construction settings without opening a connection or following later Session
      edits. It does not prove runtime TLS security. <code>TlsConfig.getVerifyingServerIdentity()</code> keeps its existing stored-choice role.</p>
    <h3>Allow for a missing common timeout when inspecting configuration</h3>
    <p class="wide">
      <code class="inline language-java">OperationalConfig.getSessionTimeout()</code> now returns nullable <code>Duration</code> instead of
      an integer millisecond count. Recompile integrations and check for null. Null means the connection, read and write settings do not
      have one known common value, not that the connection has no timeout. Previously this getter could report the builder's default even
      when the supplied Session used different values. Inspect each effective value with <code>getConnectTimeout()</code>,
      <code>getReadTimeout()</code> and <code>getWriteTimeout()</code>. The supplied <code>ConnectionConfig</code> and its builder expose
      stored choices instead; their common getter is null unless all three supplied choices are present and equal.
      Local-binding, client-hostname and certificate-trust inspection also reflects the effective construction settings rather than unrelated
      factory defaults or inactive trust choices. Later direct Session mutations are not tracked by this snapshot.
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">Duration commonTimeout = mailer.getOperationalConfig().getSessionTimeout();
if (commonTimeout != null) {
    log.info("Common socket timeout: {}", commonTimeout);
} else {
    log.info("Connect/read/write: {}/{}/{}",
        mailer.getOperationalConfig().getConnectTimeout(),
        mailer.getOperationalConfig().getReadTimeout(),
        mailer.getOperationalConfig().getWriteTimeout());
}</code></pre>
    <h3>If you call the internal Session factory directly</h3>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>You can skip this part</strong> unless your code calls
      <code class="inline language-java">MailerImpl.createMailSession(serverConfig, strategy)</code> directly. The method still exists, but it no longer
      picks up settings previously loaded through the static ConfigLoader.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      If you only used the supplied <code class="inline language-java">ServerConfig</code> and
      <code class="inline language-java">TransportStrategy</code>, nothing changes. If you expected
      <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.extraproperties.*</code> or the old global opportunistic-TLS setting to appear in the
      Session, move to the configured Mailer route below. The direct helper now enables optional STARTTLS for plain SMTP and uses only the arguments you
      pass to it.
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">Mailer configuredMailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
        .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "user", "password")
        .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP)
        .withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
            .withOpportunisticTLS(false)
            .build())
        .buildMailer();

Session configuredSession = configuredMailer.getSession();</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      Use the configured Mailer route when you want all Simple Java Mail settings applied, and close that Mailer normally when you are done. If you really
      do need a standalone Session from the internal helper, put any extra Jakarta Mail properties on that Session yourself.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#spring-context" id="spring-context" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Review Spring auto-configuration and beans</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>What breaks:</strong> If you inject the Spring bean named <code class="inline language-java">defaultMailerBuilder</code>, that injection no
      longer works. Inject <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code> and ask it for a fresh builder instead. If you only inject the default
      <code class="inline language-java">Mailer</code>, your code can stay as it is.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Spring Boot now discovers Simple Java Mail automatically whenever <code class="inline language-text">spring-module</code> is present.
      If you previously kept the module on the classpath without importing its configuration, Boot may now create mail beans automatically.
      Use the exclusion below if that application should not configure mail.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      An existing <code class="inline language-java">@Import(SimpleJavaMailSpringSupport.class)</code> is still safe and produces one bean of each type.
      Keep that import in plain Spring; Boot applications may remove it.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
      <tr><th>Bean</th><th>9.x</th><th>10.0.0</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><code class="inline language-java">defaultMailerBuilder</code></td><td><code class="inline language-java">MailerGenericBuilder&lt;?&gt;</code></td><td>Removed</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code class="inline language-java">simpleJavaMailConfig</code></td><td>Not present</td><td>Read-only <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig</code> for this Spring context</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code class="inline language-java">simpleJavaMail</code></td><td>Not present</td><td>The <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code> object you use to get builders in this context</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code class="inline language-java">defaultMailer</code></td><td><code class="inline language-java">Mailer</code></td><td><code class="inline language-java">Mailer</code>, built from this context's settings and closed with the context</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java">// 9.x
@Autowired
@Qualifier("defaultMailerBuilder")
private MailerGenericBuilder&lt;?&gt; builder;</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-java">// 10.0.0
@Autowired
private SimpleJavaMail mail;

@Bean(destroyMethod = "close")
Mailer customMailer() {
    return mail.mailerBuilder()
            .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
                .withBridgePort(7777)
                .build())
            .buildMailer();
}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="wide">
      Spring still starts with <code class="inline language-text">simplejavamail.properties</code> and lets Spring's Environment override it. Profiles,
      placeholders, environment variables, system properties, and their order are all handled by Spring. In 9.x, Simple Java Mail then read raw
      environment variables and JVM properties a second time, which could unexpectedly override Spring's result. That second pass is gone. If you relied
      on it, move the value into Spring's normal property sources.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Each application context now keeps its own config, <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code> object, and default Mailer. Starting a
      second context no longer changes the settings used by the first one.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      If you define your own <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig</code>,
      <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMail</code>, or <code class="inline language-java">Mailer</code> bean, Boot uses it instead of creating
      another bean of that type. Boot closes the Mailer it creates when the context closes; for your own Mailer bean, Spring follows that bean's shutdown settings. Use
      <code class="inline language-java">@Bean(destroyMethod = "")</code> only when the application deliberately owns shutdown elsewhere.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Automatic discovery can create these beans even when no SMTP host is configured; the default host is
      <code class="inline language-text">localhost</code>. Set <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.smtp.host</code> when sending
      elsewhere. To keep a Boot application from creating mail beans automatically, use the normal exclusion:
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-properties">spring.autoconfigure.exclude=org.simplejavamail.springsupport.SimpleJavaMailAutoConfiguration</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      For the current dependency choices, compatibility matrix, bean examples, and profile behavior, continue with the
      <a href="/spring.html">Spring integration guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#embedded-route-flags" id="embedded-route-flags" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Check two embedded-image settings</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>You can skip this section</strong> unless your Spring configuration uses either of the two settings below. If you swapped their values to
      work around the 9.x bug, put each value under the key it actually describes.
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.embeddedimages.dynamicresolution.outside.base.url=false
simplejavamail.embeddedimages.dynamicresolution.outside.base.classpath=true</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      In 9.x, Simple Java Mail accidentally mixed up the internal names for
      <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.embeddedimages.dynamicresolution.outside.base.url</code> and
      <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.embeddedimages.dynamicresolution.outside.base.classpath</code>. Regular property-file
      loading happened to cancel out the mistake, but Spring could apply each value to the other route. In 10.0.0 the URL key controls URL resolution and
      the classpath key controls classpath resolution, just as their names suggest.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#angus-api-boundary" id="angus-api-boundary" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>If your own code imports Angus</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>Most users can skip this section.</strong> Your normal Simple Java Mail dependency still includes Angus at runtime. You only need to do
      something if your own Java source imports <code class="inline language-java">org.eclipse.angus.*</code> classes.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Angus no longer appears on your compile path through Simple Java Mail and is no longer an automatic JPMS requirement of
      <code class="inline language-text">org.simplejavamail</code>. If you use Angus APIs directly, either replace those calls with standard Jakarta Mail
      or Simple Java Mail APIs, or declare Angus as your own dependency:
    </p>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;!-- Only when your own source imports Angus classes directly. --&gt;
&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.eclipse.angus&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;angus-mail&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;2.0.5&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-java">module your.application {
    requires org.simplejavamail;
    requires org.eclipse.angus.mail; // only for direct Angus API use
}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="wide">
      To replace Angus entirely, see the <a href="modules.html#mail-transport-adapter-spi">provider integration guide</a> for Maven/Gradle exclusions,
      adapter registration, content-preservation requirements, and submission results.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#provider-neutral-mime" id="provider-neutral-mime" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>If you cast converted messages to SMTPMessage</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>You can skip this section</strong> if you only use the <code class="inline language-java">MimeMessage</code> methods returned by
      <code class="inline language-java">EmailConverter.emailToMimeMessage(...)</code>. If you cast that result to Angus
      <code class="inline language-java">SMTPMessage</code>, remove the cast.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      The method still returns a normal Jakarta Mail <code class="inline language-java">MimeMessage</code>, and standard headers, recipients, and content
      work as before. The object is no longer an Angus message underneath. If you tested its concrete class or used Angus-only setters, move that work to
      the code that actually submits the message, where the mail provider is known.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#delivery-envelope" id="delivery-envelope" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>If you use bounce addresses or delivery-status notifications</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>What to check:</strong> If you set a bounce address or request delivery-status notifications, send the original
      <code class="inline language-java">Email</code> through a Simple Java Mail Mailer. That path still applies those options when submitting the email.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      A bounce address and delivery-status notification (DSN) options are instructions to the SMTP server. They are not part of the message body or
      headers. Version 10.0.0 keeps them on the <code class="inline language-java">Email</code> until it is sent. If you need to inspect them beforehand, use
      <code class="inline language-java">email.getBounceToRecipient()</code> and
      <code class="inline language-java">email.getDeliveryStatusNotification()</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      You only need to change code that converted the <code class="inline language-java">Email</code> first and then sent the resulting
      <code class="inline language-java">MimeMessage</code> through Jakarta Mail. Keep the original <code class="inline language-java">Email</code> and
      send that through your Simple Java Mail Mailer instead:
    </p>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java">// 9.x pattern that needs changing
MimeMessage message =
        EmailConverter.emailToMimeMessage(email, session);
Transport.send(message);</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-java">// 10.0.0: keep the Email until send time
mailer.sync().sendMail(email);</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="wide">
      If you keep your own Jakarta Mail sending code, read the bounce and DSN options from the <code class="inline language-java">Email</code> and apply
      them through your mail provider's API yourself. The following example maps the existing bounce address and NOTIFY/RET options, not ENVID.
      For Angus, that means
      <a href="#angus-api-boundary">declaring Angus as a compile dependency</a> and doing the following:
    </p>
    <pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">MimeMessage converted =
        EmailConverter.emailToMimeMessage(email, session);
SMTPMessage message = new SMTPMessage(converted);

Recipient bounceTo = email.getBounceToRecipient();
if (bounceTo != null) {
    message.setEnvelopeFrom(bounceTo.getAddress());
}

DeliveryStatusNotification dsn =
        email.getDeliveryStatusNotification();
if (dsn != null) {
    int notifyOptions = 0;
    for (DeliveryStatusNotification.NotifyOption option
            : dsn.getNotifyOptions()) {
        switch (option) {
            case SUCCESS:
                notifyOptions |= SMTPMessage.NOTIFY_SUCCESS;
                break;
            case FAILURE:
                notifyOptions |= SMTPMessage.NOTIFY_FAILURE;
                break;
            case DELAY:
                notifyOptions |= SMTPMessage.NOTIFY_DELAY;
                break;
            case NEVER:
                notifyOptions = SMTPMessage.NOTIFY_NEVER;
                break;
        }
    }
    if (!dsn.getNotifyOptions().isEmpty()) {
        message.setNotifyOptions(notifyOptions);
    }
    if (dsn.getReturnOption() != null) {
        message.setReturnOption(dsn.getReturnOption()
                == DeliveryStatusNotification.ReturnOption.FULL_MESSAGE
                ? SMTPMessage.RETURN_FULL
                : SMTPMessage.RETURN_HDRS);
    }
}

Transport.send(message);</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      Other mail providers need their own equivalent. This is the provider-specific work that Simple Java Mail normally handles for you. When you send
      through a Simple Java Mail Mailer, it now stops before sending if the active provider cannot honor those options. In 9.x they could be silently
      ignored.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      <strong>If your tests compare SMTP commands:</strong> When the server supports delivery-status notifications (DSNs), the bundled Angus adapter
      now includes a new identifier for each send so a later notification can refer to that attempt. SMTP calls it <code>ENVID</code>; its generated
      value is a UUID. Allow for that changing identifier in tests that compare the complete <code>MAIL FROM</code> command, even if you did not request notifications.
      Message-ID and MIME bytes are unaffected. Servers without usable DSN support still receive the email without ENVID.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Existing raw ENVID configuration suppresses automatic generation. Direct Jakarta Mail sends and CustomMailer keep their existing envelope
      handling. See the <a href="/features.html#section-dsn-envelope-id">ENVID provider boundaries</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Those connections also send the actual delivery address alongside each recipient command, so a later notification can identify the original
      recipient even if the address changes on the way. SMTP calls this parameter <code>ORCPT</code>. It includes BCC and override receivers, even when
      you have not requested notifications, without changing message bytes or adding round trips.
      Update SMTP fixtures that compare complete <code>RCPT TO</code> commands. Optional ORCPT is omitted when an address cannot be represented safely
      or exceeds the encoded parameter limit. Ordinary Angus transports obtained from caller-owned Sessions do not gain this behavior;
      custom socket factories on library-owned stock Angus Sessions retain the managed hook.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      <strong>Breaking Java API change:</strong> the <code>EmailPopulatingBuilder</code> String overloads of
      <code>withDeliveryStatusNotificationNotifyOptions</code> and <code>withDeliveryStatusNotificationReturnOption</code>, available since 9.0.0,
      are removed in 10.0.0. Use the existing enum overloads instead: replace <code>"FAILURE,DELAY"</code> with <code>NotifyOption.FAILURE, NotifyOption.DELAY</code>, and
      <code>"HDRS"</code> with <code>DeliveryStatusNotification.ReturnOption.HEADERS_ONLY</code>, then recompile.
      Property and Spring values are unchanged. CLI option names and text input also stay the same, including comma/semicolon-separated notification
      events and the <code>FULL</code>/<code>HDRS</code> return aliases. No configuration or CLI migration is needed for this change.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#async-shutdown" id="async-shutdown" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Allow shutdown to wait for sends already in progress</h2>
  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      <strong>What changes:</strong> Calling <code>Mailer.close()</code> can now wait while sends you already started finish. With the built-in executor,
      it waits for asynchronous work accepted for execution before closing the connection pool, rather than closing connections that work may still need.
      If you want to start shutdown without waiting in that call, use <code>closeAsync()</code>; its future completes after the work and
      connection cleanup finish. New async tasks are rejected once shutdown starts. Check each send's own completion result to see whether it succeeded.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Replace the removed <code>shutdownConnectionPool()</code> method with <code>closeAsync()</code>. The name now reflects that it closes the
      whole Mailer, even without pooling. The return type is <code>CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt;</code>, so existing <code>Future&lt;Void&gt;</code>
      variables can still hold it. Recompile integrations. Repeated calls return independent views of the same cleanup operation; completing or
      cancelling a view cannot interrupt cleanup or falsely complete another caller's view.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Call <code>close()</code> from application shutdown code, not from a send worker or a callback running as part of that send: that would wait for
      the same send to finish and is rejected. Waiting for sends to finish does not cancel slow sends or add a timeout.
      It also waits for this Mailer's synchronous sends and sends on an executor you supplied, without shutting that executor down.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Connection tests now reject work after shutdown begins, including a queued asynchronous test that starts after closing. Previously a closed
      Mailer could still perform a test, especially with a caller-owned executor. Build a new Mailer rather than testing through a closed one.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      See <a href="sending-and-execution.html#section-mailer-lifecycle">lifecycle guidance</a> for the complete shutdown contract.
    </p>
    <p class="wide"><code>Mailer.close()</code> now declares <code>ExecutionException</code> and <code>InterruptedException</code>, replacing the broader
      released <code>throws Exception</code> contract (inherited from <code>AutoCloseable</code> in earlier 9.x).
      If you implement Mailer yourself, narrow an override that declares broader checked exceptions. Review catches for other checked exception types
      when closing the Mailer was their only throwing call; those catches may no longer compile. The JVM method descriptor and cleanup behavior are
      unchanged by this declaration refinement. Interrupted waiting still does not cancel cleanup.</p>
    <p class="wide">
      <strong>Standalone batch-module:</strong> <code>BatchTransportExecutor.shutdown()</code> and <code>shutdownNow()</code> keep their names and
      return types, but now return a fresh completion view on each call. Released 9.3.6 returned the same mutable future. Remove assumptions about
      future identity: cancellation, manual completion, <code>orTimeout(...)</code> and <code>completeOnTimeout(...)</code> now affect only the
      view you changed. They cannot cancel or falsely finish cleanup, and a later <code>close()</code> still waits for actual cleanup.
      Use <code>shutdownNow()</code> when you intend to request forced shutdown.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      The standalone executor also rejects blocking <code>close()</code> from its own callbacks and inline completion handlers with
      <code>IllegalStateException</code>, before starting shutdown. This replaces a possible indefinite wait. Move the blocking wait to the owning
      application thread; callbacks can initiate <code>shutdown()</code> or <code>shutdownNow()</code> and return. Review
      <code>thenRun(batch::close)</code> and similar continuations because they may run on the completing worker. Waiting on the shutdown future
      within that callback is still unsupported, and supplied executors remain application-owned. See
      <a href="smtp-connection-pooling.html#option-batch-facade">standalone pooling usage</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#mail-send-execution-control" id="mail-send-execution-control" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Update stored async results to MailSend</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>If your code stores the <code>CompletableFuture</code> returned by an async send, store a <code>MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt;</code>
      instead and call <code>getCompletion()</code> to obtain the future. You can still wait for the result or attach completion handlers.
      Asynchronous simple batches return <code>MailSend&lt;Void&gt;</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">MailSend&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; send = mailer.async().sendMail(email);
CompletableFuture&lt;MailSubmissionReceipt&gt; completed = send.getCompletion();</code></pre>
    <p>The future returned by <code>getCompletion()</code> reports the result; it does not control the send. Cancelling that future, completing it
      manually, or applying <code>orTimeout(...)</code> does not stop SMTP work. Each call to <code>getCompletion()</code> returns a separate future;
      changes you make to one do not change the result observed through another.</p>
    <p>See <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-send-execution">Sending and execution</a> for the current result contract.</p>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#eml-bytes" id="eml-bytes" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>If you parse raw EML streams</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>Most applications have nothing to change.</strong> The InputStream overloads of
      <code class="inline language-java">EmailConverter.emlToEmail(...)</code> and
      <code class="inline language-java">emlToEmailBuilder(...)</code> now parse the bytes you supplied instead of first turning the whole file into a
      UTF-8 String and back again.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      Normal EML should produce the same Email. Check this only if you feed it raw non-UTF-8 data, malformed EML, or have tests that expect UTF-8
      replacement characters. Keeping the original bytes also prevents signed MIME from changing before a security module checks it. Overloads that
      accept a Java String still treat that String as UTF-8.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      MIME, EML and Outlook import now leave the bounce recipient absent when a <code>Return-Path</code> cannot be parsed, including a comment-only
      value. The original header remains in <code>Email.getHeaders()</code> and readable content is retained. Check
      <code>getBounceToRecipient()</code> for null instead of expecting malformed source text in its address. Successfully parsed values keep their
      existing interpretation; caller-built recipient behavior is unchanged.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      MIME and EML import take message-level headers, bounce addresses and receipt destinations only from the effective root message after
      protection processing. Headers on attachments, body parts or nested multipart containers no longer change those parent fields or appear
      in the parent's <code>Email.getHeaders()</code>. Attachments keep their existing resource metadata and content. If your application relied
      on child headers appearing at the message level, read the correct owning message instead; no method names or arguments change.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      MIME/EML import now recognizes <code>Disposition-Notification-To</code> and <code>Return-Receipt-To</code> regardless of capitalization.
      If you read lowercase or mixed-case versions from <code>Email.getHeaders()</code>, use
      <code>getDispositionNotificationToRecipients()</code> or <code>getReturnReceiptToRecipients()</code> and the corresponding request flag
      instead. Recognized usable receipt fields follow those typed destinations, preserving mailbox spelling, order and duplicates.
      Importing sends no receipt; value parsing and exact/protected source bytes retain their existing contracts.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      OpenPGP inspection adds <code>UNKNOWN</code> for facts that could not be established, including ordinary import without
      <code>openpgp-module</code>. A signature inside undecrypted ciphertext is unknown even when decryption reports a missing key or failure.
      Keep those cases separate from confirmed absence and actual cryptographic failure. These OpenPGP result types are new in the 10.0.0 API;
      see the <a href="security.html#section-reading-openpgp-status">OpenPGP status guide</a> for the current meanings.
    </p>
  </section>

  <a href="#finalized-mime" id="finalized-mime" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Check raw MIME tests and size limits</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide" style="font-size:smaller">
      <strong>You can skip this section</strong> unless you compare exact EML in tests or set
      <code class="inline language-java">ValidationConfig.Builder.withMaximumEmailSize(...)</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      If a test compares a complete <code class="inline language-text">.eml</code> file byte for byte, you may need to update its expected output.
      Simple Java Mail now finishes the encoded email before signing, measuring, logging or sending it. That includes headers, attachment encodings,
      the separators between message parts, Date and Message-ID. Signed messages retain the exact bytes covered by their signatures.
    </p>
    <p class="wide">
      If you set <code class="inline language-java">ValidationConfig.Builder.withMaximumEmailSize(...)</code>, the limit now applies to the complete encoded email, including
      attachment encoding and bytes added by signing or encryption. That can be larger than the original text and files, so a message close to your
      limit may now be rejected where 9.x accepted it. MIME logging shows that same encoded message.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="unparseable-imported-addresses">Inspect malformed imported address fields</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>MIME/EML conversion now keeps the complete source value when existing address interpretation fails with an address-grammar error.
      Input such as <code>Reply-To: broken &lt;address</code> can therefore import where earlier releases aborted. Readable neighboring fields,
      bodies and attachments remain available, and the retained value stays in a typed <code>Recipient</code> so normal outgoing validation
      can inspect it. Use validation or rehearsal when deciding whether an imported message is suitable for sending.</p>
    <p>Successful parsing, existing non-strict To/Cc/Bcc interpretation, names, missing-field fallbacks and singular/list behavior remain unchanged.
      This recovery does not replace malformed Reply-To with From, hide content/codec/protection failures, change Return-Path recovery or weaken
      exact EML's byte and explicit-envelope boundaries. No Java method migration is needed. See
      <a href="features.html#unparseable-imported-addresses">incoming address interpretation</a>.</p>
  </div></section>

